WN.lb(11,{"mt_OvyoRo47K-":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"You have 4, then 2 more join. Altogether…","choices":["6","4","2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Keep the starting four and add the two newcomers.","explain":"Four and two more make six altogether."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"5 red and 3 blue joined. How many in all?","choices":["8","5","3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count both colours after they share one mat.","explain":"Five and three make eight in all."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"After joining 4 and 4, the total is…","choices":["8 from putting together","0 because you must restart","only the second 4"],"answer":0,"hint":"Joining already built the total for you.","explain":"Four and four make eight; you do not wipe the first four."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"4 cars, then 3 more. Altogether…","choices":["7 cars","4 cars","3 cars"],"answer":0,"hint":"Parked cars plus the friend’s cars.","explain":"Four and three more make seven cars."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Join {a} blocks and {b} blocks. What is the total?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add both block counts for the combined total.","explain":"{a} plus {b} is {=a+b} blocks.","params":{"a":[1,6],"b":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A story starts with {a} apples. {b} more are put with them. How many apples?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Combine the start amount with the extras.","explain":"{a} and {b} more make {=a+b} apples.","params":{"a":[2,7],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Why join two mats?","choices":["to find how many altogether","to hide the first group","to make the total smaller on purpose"],"answer":0,"hint":"The point of combining is the new total count.","explain":"Joining shows how many there are in all."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which is an addition (put together) story?","choices":["3 birds, then 2 more land","5 sweets, eat 2 of them","a line of 4 with none added"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for more arriving, not some leaving.","explain":"More landing is put-together addition; eating is take-away."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Putting 3 and 2 together makes…","choices":["5 altogether","still only 3","a smaller group than 3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Keep both piles when you slide them into one.","explain":"Three and two combined make five altogether."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Addition means…","choices":["putting groups together to find how many in all","making a group smaller","only saying the first group’s number"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of two mats becoming one total.","explain":"Addition finds how many altogether after combining."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"You join {a} counters and {b} counters. How many altogether?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the two group sizes to get the total.","explain":"{a} and {b} make {=a+b} altogether.","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"You start with {a} cars. {b} more arrive. How many cars now?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Start amount plus the newcomers.","explain":"{a} cars and {b} more make {=a+b} cars.","params":{"a":[3,6],"b":[1,3]}},{"type":"order","id":"p5","prompt":"Order the steps for combining two groups.","items":["see two groups","put them together","say how many in all"],"hint":"Look first, join next, then name the total.","explain":"See the groups, join them, then say the total."}],"explain":{"kid":["Addition means put two groups together.","Count how many there are in all.","Keep both groups. Do not throw the first away.","4 and 3 more make 7 altogether."],"adult":["EYFS / Y1 addition (NC Number; CCSS K.OA): addition as combining / putting together / add-to. The total is how many altogether.","Common mistakes: recounting from one as if the first group vanished; saying only the second amount; mixing addition with take-away.","In the lab your child joins mats, adds more to a start amount, says the total, resists a restart trap, and acts a car story."]},"familyTip":"Put 4 spoons in one pile and 3 in another. Ask: 'How many altogether if we put them together?' Then slide the piles into one and count once.","keyIdea":"Addition means putting two groups together and finding how many there are altogether. The total keeps both amounts — nothing is thrown away.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Combining — fair idea or myth? Drag each clue, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Fair idea","Myth / mix-up"],"items":[["addition means putting groups together",0],["joining 3 and 2 makes 5 altogether",0],["after joining you must pretend the first group vanished",1],["4 cars and 3 more make 7 cars",0],["addition always means take some away",1],["the total keeps both amounts",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagaddtog","caption":"Flagship lab: join two mats, add-to a group, say the total, catch restart myths, act a car story, then challenge.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_zuKAX6lcYR":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"The minus sign in 7 − 3 means…","choices":["take away","put together","skip counting only"],"answer":0,"hint":"Minus marks the take-away / remove action.","explain":"− means take away in early subtraction sentences."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Saying 'how many left' is 3 when you removed 3 from 7…","choices":["mixes up the removed count with the remainder","is always correct","means you should add next"],"answer":0,"hint":"The removed number is not the same as how many remain.","explain":"Removed = 3; left = 4. Do not swap them."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Combining two groups is…","choices":["addition, not taking away","the same as subtraction","how minus always works"],"answer":0,"hint":"Putting together finds a total; taking away finds what remains.","explain":"Combining is addition. Taking away is subtraction."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"9 take away 2 leaves…","choices":["7","2","11"],"answer":0,"hint":"Start at 9, remove 2, count the rest.","explain":"Start with 9, remove 2, and 7 remain on the plate."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Start with {n}. Take away {k}. What remains?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Subtract k from n for how many are left.","explain":"{n} take away {k} leaves {=n-k} as the remainder.","params":{"n":[6,10],"k":[2,5]},"where":"n>k"},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A box holds {n} blocks. You remove {k}. How many blocks stay?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Stay means the remainder after taking away.","explain":"{n} take away {k} leaves {=n-k} blocks.","params":{"n":[5,9],"k":[1,4]},"where":"n>k"},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Why act take-away with objects first?","choices":["so you can see the group shrink and count what remains","so you can skip the minus sign forever","so addition and subtraction mean the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Concrete remove-and-count builds the meaning of left.","explain":"Objects make the take-away visible before the written sentence."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Changing the starting total while you remove…","choices":["breaks a fair take-away","is required every time","only helps addition"],"answer":0,"hint":"Keep the start fixed, then remove, then count left.","explain":"A fair take-away keeps the starting amount clear before removing."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Taking away means…","choices":["remove some, then count how many remain","add more to the group","only name the colour of the plate"],"answer":0,"hint":"Start with a group, take some off, look at what is left.","explain":"Subtraction as taking away finds how many remain after some are removed."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"7 biscuits, eat 3. How many left?","choices":["4","3","10"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do not answer with the 3 eaten. Count what stays on the plate.","explain":"7 take away 3 leaves 4. The removed count is not 'how many left'."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"You start with {n} toys and take away {k}. How many are left?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Subtract the number removed from the starting number.","explain":"{n} take away {k} leaves {=n-k} toys still there.","params":{"n":[5,9],"k":[1,4]},"where":"n>k"},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A plate has {n} grapes. You eat {k}. How many grapes remain?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Remain means how many are still on the plate after eating.","explain":"{n} take away {k} leaves {=n-k}.","params":{"n":[4,8],"k":[1,3]},"where":"n>k"},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"After taking away, putting the removed objects back…","choices":["undoes the subtraction","is required to find how many left","always makes the answer bigger than the start"],"answer":0,"hint":"How many left is counted after the remove step, not after putting them back.","explain":"Adding removed objects back cancels the take-away."}],"explain":{"kid":["Taking away means you start with some things, remove some, and see how many are left.","If you have 7 biscuits and eat 3, 4 biscuits stay on the plate.","We write that as 7 − 3 = 4. The minus sign means take away.","Do not put the eaten biscuits back when you answer. Do not say the answer is only the 3 you removed."],"adult":["EYFS / Year 1 Maths: subtraction as taking away / separating from a group; find how many remain. Words: take away, left, remain, minus, subtract, how many left.","Common mistakes: adding removed objects back; giving the removed count as 'how many left'; mixing take-away with combining (addition); changing the starting total mid-count.","In the lab your child removes counters, reads the remainder, rejects add-back and removed=left myths, builds a take-away number sentence, then faces challenge traps for those slips."]},"familyTip":"Put 7 biscuits on a plate. Eat or hide 3. Ask: 'How many are left?' Say together: '7 take away 3 is 4.' Act it with toys before writing 7 − 3 = 4.","keyIdea":"Subtraction as taking away means start with a group, remove some, and count how many remain. The minus sign marks the take-away. Do not add the removed objects back, and do not count only what you removed as the answer for how many are left.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Taking away or not? Drag each clue, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Taking away","Not taking away"],"items":[["start with 7, remove 3, count what remains",0],["add the removed biscuits back onto the plate",1],["say 7 take away 3 is 4",0],["report only the 3 you removed as how many are left",1],["write 7 − 3 = 4 after acting it out",0],["put more on the plate instead of removing",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagtake","caption":"Flagship lab: remove counters from a group, read how many left, catch add-back and removed=left slips, write a take-away sentence, then challenge.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_8RmpkDxT9L":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"The − sign means…","choices":["take away / subtract","put together","draw a line only"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of removing counters from a group.","explain":"Minus means take away or subtract."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"How do you read 4 + 1 = 5?","choices":["four plus one equals five","four take away one equals five","four answer-box five"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say plus for + and equals for =.","explain":"4 + 1 = 5 reads as four plus one equals five."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Is 5 = 2 + 3 a fair sentence?","choices":["yes — both sides are the same","no — the answer must sit on the right","no — equals only means take away"],"answer":0,"hint":"Check whether both sides match in value.","explain":"5 and 2 + 3 are the same value, so the sentence is fair."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"In 6 − 1 = 5, the − means…","choices":["take away","put together","is the same as"],"answer":0,"hint":"One counter is removed from the group of six.","explain":"The minus sign marks take-away."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"You matched {n} signs to their meanings. How many matches?","answer":"n","hint":"That match count is the answer.","explain":"You made {n} correct sign matches.","params":{"n":[3,8]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"{n} cards show +, − or =. {k} show equals. How many show + or −?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Subtract the equals cards from the pack.","explain":"{n} minus {k} equals cards leaves {=n-k} plus or minus cards.","params":{"n":[6,10],"k":[1,4]},"where":"n>k"},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Why learn the three signs?","choices":["so number sentences can be read and written clearly","so every sum hides the first number","because = is only a decoration"],"answer":0,"hint":"Signs are the words of maths sentences.","explain":"Plus, minus and equals let you read and write number sentences."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which reading is correct for 7 − 2 = 5?","choices":["seven minus two equals five","seven plus two equals five","seven answer-box five"],"answer":0,"hint":"Use minus for − and equals for =.","explain":"7 − 2 = 5 is seven minus two equals five."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"The + sign means…","choices":["put together / add","take away","the answer box only"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of two groups sliding into one total.","explain":"Plus means put together or add."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"The = sign means…","choices":["is the same as","always put the answer on the right","take away"],"answer":0,"hint":"Both sides should balance in value.","explain":"Equals means both sides have the same value."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"You read {n} number sentences aloud. How many did you read?","answer":"n","hint":"Count each sentence you said in words.","explain":"You read {n} number sentences.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A sheet has {n} signs. {k} are plus signs. How many are not plus?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Subtract the plus signs from the total.","explain":"{n} signs minus {k} plus leaves {=n-k} other signs.","params":{"n":[5,9],"k":[1,4]},"where":"n>k"},{"type":"order","id":"p5","prompt":"Order the words for reading 2 + 3 = 5.","items":["two plus","three equals","five"],"hint":"Start with the first number and the plus.","explain":"two plus, three equals, five."}],"explain":{"kid":["Plus (+) means put together.","Minus (−) means take away.","Equals (=) means is the same as.","Both sides of = must match in value."],"adult":["NC Y1 Number / CCSS 1.OA: read and interpret +, − and = in number sentences. Equals means sameness of value, not a cue to write an answer on the right.","Common mistakes: reading + as take-away; treating = as an answer box; rejecting sentences like 5 = 2 + 3.","In the lab your child names each sign, reads 3 + 2 = 5 in words, accepts 5 = 3 + 2 as fair, then tries the challenge."]},"familyTip":"Write 4 + 2 = 6 and 6 = 4 + 2 on a whiteboard. Ask your child to read both aloud and say why both are fair.","keyIdea":"The + sign means put together (add). The − sign means take away (subtract). The = sign means is the same as — both sides have the same value, not just “write the answer on the right”.","visual":{"type":"symbols","caption":"Choose + or −, change the numbers, and read the sentence. The bottom row shows what = means.","opts":{"a":7,"b":2,"op":"-"}},"lab":{"type":"flagpmeq","caption":"Flagship lab: name +, − and =, read a sentence aloud, catch the answer-box myth, then challenge.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_oIzycTBeE4":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"10 = 6 + □. What goes in the box?","answer":4,"hint":"6 plus what makes 10?","explain":"6 + 4 = 10."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"5 + 5 = 3 + □. What goes in the box?","answer":7,"hint":"The left side is 10.","explain":"5 + 5 = 10 and 3 + 7 = 10."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Someone writes 8 + 4 = 12 + 5 = 17. What's wrong?","choices":["8 + 4 is not the same as 12 + 5, so the first = is false","Nothing is wrong","The answer should be 16"],"answer":0,"hint":"Check whether each pair of sides joined by = really is equal.","explain":"8 + 4 = 12, which is not 12 + 5. Each = must join two things of equal value."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"{a} + {b} = {c} + □. What goes in the box?","answer":"a+b-c","hint":"Work out the left side first.","explain":"The left side is {=a+b}, and {c} + {=a+b-c} = {=a+b}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[2,9],"c":[1,8]},"where":"a+b>c"},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"True or false: {a} + {b} = {b} + {a}","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"hint":"Work out each side.","explain":"Both sides make {=a+b}, so it's true.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[2,9]},"where":"a!=b"},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"True or false: {a} + {b} = {=a+b+1}","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"hint":"Work out the left side.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b}, not {=a+b+1}, so it's false.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"{a} = {b} + □. What goes in the box?","answer":"a-b","hint":"{b} plus what makes {a}?","explain":"{b} + {=a-b} = {a}.","params":{"a":[5,15],"b":[1,9]},"where":"a>b"}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"True or false: 3 + 4 = 5 + 2","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"hint":"Work out each side separately.","explain":"Both sides equal 7, so it is true."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"{a} + {b} = □ + {c}. What number goes in the box?","answer":"a+b-c","hint":"The left side makes {=a+b}. What plus {c} makes {=a+b}?","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} and {=a+b-c} + {c} = {=a+b}, so the box is {=a+b-c}.","params":{"a":[3,9],"b":[2,9],"c":[1,9]},"where":"a+b>c"},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"True or false: 9 = 4 + 5","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"hint":"The sum can be on either side.","explain":"4 + 5 = 9, so it is true."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"True or false: 2 + 6 = 6 + 3","choices":["True","False"],"answer":1,"hint":"Work out each side.","explain":"2 + 6 = 8 but 6 + 3 = 9, so it is false."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"True or false: {a} + {b} = {=a+1} + {=b-1}","choices":["True","False"],"answer":0,"hint":"Work out each side.","explain":"Both sides make {=a+b}, so it is true.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[2,9]}}],"explain":{"kid":["= means both sides are worth the same, like a balanced seesaw.","3 + 4 = 7 is true, and so is 7 = 3 + 4. Even 3 + 4 = 5 + 2 is true, because both sides make 7."],"adult":["The equals sign states a relationship ('is the same as'), not an instruction to calculate. Many learners read = as 'and the answer is', which leads to errors like 8 + 4 = □ + 5, then writing 12 in the box.","Asking whether sentences are true or false (9 = 4 + 5, 2 + 6 = 6 + 3) builds the balanced view of equations that algebra depends on."]},"familyTip":"Use a seesaw or kitchen scales: 'Is this side the same as that side?' Swap which side the answer is on.","keyIdea":"= means both sides are worth the same, like a balanced seesaw.","visual":{"type":"balance","caption":"Make the right side the same value as the left. The scale levels when both sides are equal.","opts":{"mode":"equal","left":[3,4],"right":5}}},"mt_7XcCG43ZZW":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"What goes with {n} to make 10?","answer":"10-n","hint":"Hold up {n} fingers. How many fingers are still down?","explain":"{n} and {=10-n} make 10. The fingers still down show {=10-n}.","params":{"n":[1,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"A train has {w} cubes. You break off {k}. How many cubes are in the other part?","answer":"w-k","hint":"The two parts join back to make {w}. Count on from {k} to {w}.","explain":"{k} and {=w-k} make {w}, so the other part has {=w-k} cubes.","params":{"w":[6,10],"k":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which pair makes 5?","choices":["2 and 3","2 and 2","5 and 5"],"answer":0,"hint":"Join the two parts. Do you get 5?","explain":"2 and 3 make 5. 2 and 2 make 4, and 5 and 5 make 10."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"9 bears in all. You see 6. Sam says 15 are hiding. What went wrong?","choices":["He added instead of finding the missing part","Nothing went wrong","He counted the bears he could see"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can more bears hide than there are in all?","explain":"9 is all the bears, so the hidden part is less than 9. Count on from 6 to 9: 3 bears are hiding."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"How many different ways can you break a train of 4 cubes into 2 parts?","choices":["3","4","2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Try every gap: after 1 cube, after 2 cubes, after 3 cubes.","explain":"A 4-cube train has 3 gaps: 1 and 3, 2 and 2, 3 and 1. So there are 3 ways."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"In a part-whole model the whole is 8 and one part is 5. What is the other part?","choices":["3","13","4"],"answer":0,"hint":"The two parts must join to make the whole, 8.","explain":"5 and 3 make 8, so the other part is 3. 13 is too big, because a part is smaller than the whole."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which is the same as 3 and 7?","choices":["7 and 3","3 and 6","4 and 7"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the same two numbers, turned around.","explain":"7 and 3 is 3 and 7 turned around. Both make 10."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Ten frame: 6 red and some yellow fill it. How many yellow?","choices":["4","6","10"],"answer":0,"hint":"The red and yellow dots together fill all 10 spaces.","explain":"6 and 4 make 10, so there are 4 yellow. 6 is the red and 10 is all of them."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What goes with 7 to make 10?","choices":["3","4","17"],"answer":0,"hint":"Put 7 dots in a ten frame. How many spaces are still empty?","explain":"7 and 3 make 10. Count on from 7: 8, 9, 10. That is 3 more."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A ten frame has {n} dots. How many more dots fill it up to 10?","answer":"10-n","hint":"Count the empty spaces, or count on from {n} to 10.","explain":"{n} and {=10-n} make 10, so {=10-n} more dots fill the frame.","params":{"n":[1,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"There are {w} bears in all. You can see {s}. How many are under the cup?","answer":"w-s","hint":"Start at {s} and count on until you say {w}.","explain":"{s} and {=w-s} make {w}, so {=w-s} bears are hiding under the cup.","params":{"w":[5,10],"s":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"2 and 4 make 6. What do 4 and 2 make?","choices":["6","7","2"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is the same two parts, just turned around.","explain":"Turning the parts around does not change the whole. 4 and 2 make 6, just like 2 and 4."}],"explain":{"kid":["Make a train of 6 cubes. Break it into two bits. You might get 2 and 4. Join it up and break it again. Now you might get 1 and 5. Both make 6.","A ten frame has 10 spaces. Put in 7 red dots. Fill the gaps with yellow. You need 3 yellow. 7 and 3 make 10.","Hide some bears under a cup. There are 8 in all. You see 5. Count on from 5: 6, 7, 8. That is 3 more. So 3 bears are under the cup.","Turn the train around. 2 and 4 is the same as 4 and 2. It is still 6 cubes. Nothing was added and nothing went away."],"adult":["These are number bonds: the pairs of numbers that make a given whole. US Common Core K.OA.A.3 asks kindergarten children to decompose numbers up to 10 into pairs in more than one way, and K.OA.A.4 asks them to find the number that makes 10 with any number from 1 to 9. In England, the Early Learning Goal for Number expects automatic recall of number bonds up to 5 and some to 10, and the Year 1 programme of study says to represent and use number bonds and related subtraction facts within 20.","The key idea is part-whole: a whole can be partitioned into parts, and the parts recombine to make the same whole. The cherry diagram (a part-whole model) shows this with the whole in the top circle and the two parts below. The ten frame helps because a child can see the empty spaces, so the bond to 10 is the gap. Turning a pair around is commutativity: 3 + 7 and 7 + 3 make the same total, which halves the facts a child needs to learn.","Common mistakes: adding the two numbers given instead of finding the missing part, so 9 in all with 6 showing gives 15; naming the part you can see, so the answer is 6; being one out when counting on, because the child counts the starting number again; and treating 4 and 6 as a new fact after learning 6 and 4. Ask how the child knows, and check the parts by joining them back up.","In the lab your child breaks a 6-cube train at every gap to find all five ways, fills ten frames that start with 7, 4 and 9 red counters, works out how many bears hide under a cup, builds three part-whole models for 10, and turns cube trains around to see that the total stays the same."]},"familyTip":"Use 10 small toys and a cup. Hide some under the cup and ask how many are hiding. Then swap jobs. Count the gaps in an egg box with 10 spaces, or hold up 7 fingers and ask how many more make 10.","keyIdea":"A number can be split into two parts in more than one way. 6 is 1 and 5, 2 and 4, or 3 and 3. These pairs are number bonds. The two parts together make the whole. If you know the whole and one part, count on to find the other part. Turn a pair around, 2 and 4 or 4 and 2, and it still makes the same whole.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does each pair make 10? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Makes 10","Not 10"],"items":[["7 and 3",0],["6 and 3",1],["5 and 5",0],["8 and 1",1],["9 and 1",0],["4 and 4",1],["2 and 8",0],["6 and 5",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagbond","caption":"Flagship lab: break a cube train every way, fill ten frames, find bears under a cup, build part-whole models and turn pairs around.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_ezc2m_0dzN":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"A bus has 10 seats. {a} children are on it. How many more can get on?","answer":"10-a","hint":"Count up from {a} to 10, or think {a} + ? = 10.","explain":"{a} + {=10-a} = 10, so {=10-a} more can get on. 10 − {a} = {=10-a}.","params":{"a":[2,8]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"□ + {a} = {b}. What number goes in the box?","answer":"b-a","hint":"The box is the missing part. Count up from {a} to {b}.","explain":"{=b-a} and {a} make {b}, so the box is {=b-a}.","params":{"a":[3,6],"b":[9,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"You know 6 + 4 = 10. What is 10 − 6?","choices":["16","10","4"],"answer":2,"hint":"6 and 4 make 10. Take the 6 away. Which part is left?","explain":"6 + 4 = 10, so taking 6 from 10 leaves the other part: 10 − 6 = 4."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Ali works out 12 − 9 by counting up: 9, 10, 11, 12. He says 4. What went wrong?","choices":["He counted 9 as a hop","He should add 12 and 9","Nothing, 4 is right"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count only the hops: 9 to 10, 10 to 11, 11 to 12.","explain":"He counted the start number. There are 3 hops, so 12 − 9 = 3."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which is the quick way to do 15 − 13?","choices":["Count back 13 hops from 15","Count up from 13 to 15","Add 15 and 13"],"answer":1,"hint":"The two numbers are close together.","explain":"13 to 15 is only 2 hops, so 15 − 13 = 2. Counting back 13 hops gets there too, but takes much longer."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Sam says 8 + □ = 10 has 18 in the box. Is he right?","choices":["No, the box is 2","Yes, 8 and 10 make 18","No, the box is 10"],"answer":0,"hint":"Put his answer back: is 8 + 18 the same as 10?","explain":"8 + 18 is far more than 10. The box is 2, because 8 + 2 = 10."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"7 + 5 = 12. Which take-away is also true?","choices":["12 − 5 = 7","12 − 5 = 12","7 − 5 = 12"],"answer":0,"hint":"Take one part away from the whole, 12. What part is left?","explain":"12 is the whole and 7 and 5 are the parts. Take away 5 and 7 is left: 12 − 5 = 7."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"There are 9 apples in a box that holds 12. How many more fit?","choices":["3","21","9"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count up from 9 to 12 and count the hops.","explain":"9, then 10, 11, 12: 3 hops. 9 + 3 = 12, so 3 more apples fit."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"{a} + □ = 10. What number goes in the box?","answer":"10-a","hint":"Start on {a} and count up to 10. Count the hops.","explain":"{a} and {=10-a} make 10, so the box is {=10-a}. That also means 10 − {a} = {=10-a}.","params":{"a":[3,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"What is {b} − {a}? Think: {a} + ? = {b}.","answer":"b-a","hint":"Put your finger on {a} on a number track and hop up to {b}. How many hops?","explain":"From {a} up to {b} is {=b-a} hops, so {b} − {a} = {=b-a}.","params":{"a":[6,9],"b":[11,13]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which question asks the same thing as 10 − 7?","choices":["7 + □ = 10","10 + 7 = □","7 − 10 = □"],"answer":0,"hint":"10 − 7 asks: what do I add to 7 to get back to 10?","explain":"7 + □ = 10 asks what joins 7 to make 10. The answer is 3 for both: 10 − 7 = 3."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"9 = 4 + □. What goes in the box?","choices":["13","5","9"],"answer":1,"hint":"= means 'is the same as'. 4 and what make 9?","explain":"4 and 5 make 9, so both sides are the same: 9 = 4 + 5. 13 comes from adding 9 and 4, which is not the question."}],"explain":{"kid":["A bus has 10 seats. 8 kids are on. How many more can get on? Count up from 8: 9, 10. That is 2 more. So 8 + 2 = 10.","Now think of it the other way. 10 seats, and 8 are taken. 10 take away 8 is 2. It is the same 2. 10 − 8 and 8 + ? = 10 ask the same thing.","When the two numbers are close, count up. For 12 − 9, start on 9 and hop: 10, 11, 12. Count the hops, not the start. That is 3 hops, so 12 − 9 = 3.","Facts you know help too. If you know 7 + 5 = 12, then you know 12 − 7 = 5. The box in 9 = 4 + □ is the part that is missing: 5."],"adult":["This is subtraction as an unknown addend. The US Common Core puts it in Grade 1 (1.OA.4, 'understand subtraction as an unknown-addend problem', for example 'subtract 10 − 8 by finding the number that makes 10 when added to 8'), and 1.OA.8 asks children to find the unknown in equations such as 8 + ? = 11 and 5 = □ − 3. The English national curriculum asks Year 1 pupils to 'solve missing number problems such as 7 = □ − 9' and to use related subtraction facts within 20.","The big idea is the part–whole relationship: two parts make a whole, so if you know the whole and one part, the missing part is the whole minus the known part. That is why one addition fact (6 + 4 = 10) gives a fact family of two subtractions (10 − 6 = 4, 10 − 4 = 6). Counting up from the smaller number to the larger one, often called finding the difference, is far quicker than counting back when the numbers are close: 15 − 13 is 2 hops up rather than 13 hops back.","Common mistakes to watch for: adding the two numbers you can see (writing 18 for 8 + □ = 10); giving back the known part or the whole instead of the missing part; counting the start number as a hop, so 12 − 9 comes out as 4; and reading the equals sign as 'the answer comes next', so 9 = 4 + □ gets 13. Say '= means is the same as' and check by putting the answer back in the gap.","In the lab your child fills empty seats on a bus and sees both number sentences, matches each take-away to the add-on question that asks the same thing, hops a frog up a number track and counts the hops, uses a known fact to answer a take-away, and fills the box on a balance until both sides are level."]},"familyTip":"At snack time, line up 10 grapes in two rows of five and eat some while your child looks away. Ask, 'How many did I eat?' Let them count up from what is left to 10. Then say the take-away out loud together: '10 take away 7 is 3.' Try it with 9 or 12 grapes too.","keyIdea":"A take-away can be turned into an add-on question. 10 − 8 = ? asks the same thing as 8 + ? = 10: what do I add to 8 to make 10? So you can use adding to subtract. Start on the smaller number and count up to the bigger one, or use an adding fact you already know: 6 + 4 = 10 tells you 10 − 6 = 4 and 10 − 4 = 6.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is the missing part 2 or 3? Count up from the small number. Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Missing part is 2","Missing part is 3"],"items":[["10 − 8",0],["9 − 6",1],["7 − 5",0],["7 − 4",1],["12 − 10",0],["11 − 8",1],["5 + □ = 7",0],["6 + □ = 9",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagcountup","caption":"Flagship lab: fill the empty seats on a bus, match take-aways to add-on questions, count up on a number track, use a fact you know, and level a balance.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_QCgbiVrwnp":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"Work out {a} + {b}. Tip: start on the bigger number.","answer":"a+b","hint":"Start on {b}, then count on {a} more.","explain":"Start on {b} and count on {a}: that lands on {=a+b}. So {a} + {b} = {=a+b}.","params":{"a":[1,3],"b":[7,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"{a} + 6 + {=10-a}. Find two numbers that make 10 first. What is the total?","answer":"16","hint":"{a} and {=10-a} make 10. Then add the 6.","explain":"{a} + {=10-a} = 10, and 10 + 6 = 16. Adding in a different order gives the same total, 16.","params":{"a":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A domino has 4 dots on one side and 6 on the other. You turn it round. How many dots are there now?","choices":["10","2","46"],"answer":0,"hint":"Turning the domino round does not add or take away any dots.","explain":"There are still 10 dots. 4 + 6 = 10 and 6 + 4 = 10. 46 puts the digits side by side."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which one does NOT give the same answer as 7 + 2?","choices":["2 + 7","7 − 2","1 + 8"],"answer":1,"hint":"Work out each one. 7 + 2 makes 9.","explain":"7 − 2 is a take-away and makes 5, not 9. 2 + 7 is the turnaround, and 1 + 8 also makes 9."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Leo works out 2 + 8 by starting on 2 and counting on 8. What is a quicker way?","choices":["Start on 8 and count on 2","Count all from 1","Start on 2 and count on 10"],"answer":0,"hint":"You can add in any order. Which number is bigger?","explain":"Start on 8 and count on 2: 9, 10. Only 2 hops instead of 8, and the answer is still 10."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Mia says, 'I know 5 + 9 = 14, but I need to work out 9 + 5 again.' What is true?","choices":["9 + 5 is 14 too, the same numbers turned round","9 + 5 is 4","9 + 5 is 95"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did either number change when the sum was turned round?","explain":"9 + 5 = 14, because it uses the same two numbers. She does not need to work it out again."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Sam has 3 stickers. He says he can give away 5 because 5 − 3 = 2. What is wrong?","choices":["He cannot take 5 away from 3, so take-away does not turn round","Nothing, he will have 2 left","He will have 8 left"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count out 3 stickers. Can you give 5 of them away?","explain":"He runs out after 3. 5 − 3 = 2 is true, but 3 − 5 is not the same, because take-away does not turn round."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b}. So what is {b} + {a}?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Turning a sum round does not change the total. The numbers are the same, just swapped.","explain":"{b} + {a} uses the same two numbers as {a} + {b}, so it is {=a+b} too.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[3,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"{a} + {b}. Start on {b} and count on. How many hops do you make?","answer":"a","hint":"Start on the bigger number, {b}. Count on the smaller number, one hop at a time.","explain":"Start on {b} and hop {a} times to land on {=a+b}. That is only {a} hops, not {b}.","params":{"a":[1,3],"b":[6,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which sum is the same as 4 + 7 turned round?","choices":["7 + 4","7 − 4","4 + 4"],"answer":0,"hint":"Keep both numbers. Just swap their places.","explain":"7 + 4 is 4 + 7 turned round. Both make 11. 7 − 4 is a take-away, and 4 + 4 changes a number."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Is 6 − 2 the same as 2 − 6?","choices":["Yes, both are 4","No, take-away does not turn round","Yes, both are 8"],"answer":1,"hint":"Can you take 6 counters away if you only have 2?","explain":"No. 6 − 2 = 4, but you cannot take 6 away from 2. Adding turns round; take-away does not."}],"explain":{"kid":["Look at a domino with 3 dots and 8 dots. 3 and 8 make 11. Now turn it round. It is 8 and 3. The dots did not change, so it still makes 11.","That means you can pick the order. For 2 + 9, start on 9. Then count on just 2: 10, 11. That is much quicker than 9 hops from 2.","Each sum has a turnaround buddy. 4 + 7 turns round to 7 + 4. Keep both numbers and swap them. The total stays the same. With three numbers, add the easy two first. For 7 + 5 + 3, do 7 + 3 = 10. Then 10 + 5 = 15.","Take-away does not turn round. You can take 3 away from 5 and have 2 left. Try to take 5 away from 3. You run out!"],"adult":["This is the commutative property of addition: a + b = b + a. The US Common Core asks Grade 1 children to 'apply properties of operations as strategies to add and subtract', with the examples 'if 8 + 3 = 11 is known, then 3 + 8 = 11 is also known' (commutative) and '2 + 6 + 4 = 2 + 10 = 12' (associative) (1.OA.3). The English national curriculum asks Year 2 pupils to 'show that addition of two numbers can be done in any order (commutative) and subtraction of one number from another cannot'.","The practical pay-off is choosing the order that is easiest. Counting on from the larger addend (researchers call it the 'min' strategy, because the child counts on the smaller number) roughly halves the facts a child needs to learn and cuts the counting: 2 + 9 becomes two hops from 9 rather than nine hops from 2. With three numbers, children can add any two first, so they look for a pair that makes 10, a number bond they already know. The total never changes; only the effort does.","Common mistakes to watch for: believing that 8 + 3 needs working out again after 3 + 8; always starting on the first number written, even when it is small; carrying the idea over to subtraction and saying 3 − 5 = 2; writing the digits side by side, so 8 + 3 becomes 83; and counting the start number as a hop. Ask 'did the dots change?' when a child turns a sum round, and 'can you take 5 counters from 3?' when they swap a take-away. Negative numbers come much later, so for now 3 − 5 simply cannot be done with counters.","In the lab your child turns dominoes round, chooses the bigger number to count on from, finds the turnaround card for a sum (and turns down a take-away decoy), takes counters away both ways round, and adds three numbers by making 10 first."]},"familyTip":"Use a handful of buttons or pasta. Put 2 in one hand and 6 in the other, and ask how many there are. Then swap your hands round and ask again. Does it change? Next, show 6 and ask your child to take away 2, then hand them 2 and ask them to take away 6.","keyIdea":"You can add two numbers in any order and get the same total: 3 + 8 = 11 and 8 + 3 = 11. So when you count on, start on the bigger number and count on the smaller one. Take-away is different: 5 − 3 = 2, but you cannot take 5 away from 3, so a take-away does not turn round.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Do the two sides give the same answer? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Same answer","Not the same"],"items":[["3 + 8 and 8 + 3",0],["5 − 2 and 2 − 5",1],["6 + 4 and 4 + 6",0],["9 − 1 and 1 − 9",1],["2 + 7 and 7 + 2",0],["8 − 3 and 3 − 8",1],["1 + 5 and 5 + 1",0],["7 − 6 and 6 − 7",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagaddord","caption":"Flagship lab: turn dominoes round, start on the bigger number, find turnaround facts, try take-aways both ways and make 10 first.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_e8CZ7E5qW7":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"5 + 5 =","choices":["10","55","0"],"answer":0,"hint":"Add the values; do not stick the digits together.","explain":"Five and five make ten. 55 would be sticking digits."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"8 needs what to make 10?","choices":["2","8","18"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count on from 8: 9, 10 — think about making ten.","explain":"8 + 2 = 10 That is how bonds to 10 work."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Why learn bonds to 10?","choices":["so you can make 10 quickly without counting all the counters","so you never need numbers again","because 10 is an unlucky number"],"answer":0,"hint":"Bonds are a shortcut for making ten.","explain":"Known pairs let you make 10 fast, which helps later with bigger sums."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"9 + 2 =","choices":["11, which is not a bond to 10","10","92"],"answer":0,"hint":"Add 9 and 2 carefully — think about making ten.","explain":"9 + 2 = 11. The bond partner of 9 for 10 is 1, not 2."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Partner of 1 to make 10?","choices":["9","1","10"],"answer":0,"hint":"Ask what you add to 1 so the total is exactly ten.","explain":"1 + 9 = 10 That is how bonds to 10 work."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"6 + □ = 10. The box is…","choices":["4","6","16"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the number that pairs with 6 to make exactly ten.","explain":"6 + 4 = 10 That is how bonds to 10 work."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Partner of {n} to make 10?","answer":"10-n","hint":"Subtract {n} from 10 — think about making ten.","explain":"10 − {n} = {=10-n} That is how bonds to 10 work.","params":{"n":[1,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"A ten-frame shows {n} full. How many more to fill it?","answer":"10-n","hint":"Empty spaces are 10 minus the full ones.","explain":"10 − {n} = {=10-n} more counters fill the frame.","params":{"n":[3,9]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"7 + ? = 10","choices":["3","7","17"],"answer":0,"hint":"How many empty spaces if 7 are full in a ten-frame?","explain":"7 and 3 are partners: 7 + 3 = 10."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Which pair makes 10?","choices":["4 + 6","4 + 4","5 + 6"],"answer":0,"hint":"Add the two numbers and check for exactly 10.","explain":"4 + 6 = 10. 4 + 4 = 8 and 5 + 6 = 11."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"The partner of 9 to make 10 is…","choices":["1","9","10"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count on from nine until you reach ten: that count is the partner.","explain":"9 + 1 = 10, so the partner is 1."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"What number makes 10 with {n}?","answer":"10-n","hint":"Take {n} away from 10, or count on from {n} to 10.","explain":"10 − {n} = {=10-n}, so {n} + {=10-n} = 10.","params":{"n":[1,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"{n} counters are in a ten-frame. How many spaces are empty?","answer":"10-n","hint":"A ten-frame has 10 spaces. Subtract the full ones.","explain":"10 − {n} = {=10-n} empty spaces.","params":{"n":[2,8]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Some pairs of numbers add to 10. We call them number bonds to 10.","Put 7 counters in a ten-frame. Count the empty spaces. There are 3. So 7 and 3 are partners: 7 + 3 = 10.","Other pairs: 1 + 9, 2 + 8, 4 + 6, 5 + 5. Swap them and they still make 10: 9 + 1, 8 + 2, and so on.","When you see 6 + □ = 10, the empty box needs 4, because 6 and 4 are a bond to 10."],"adult":["Reception and Year 1 number bonds ask children to find the number that makes 10 with a given 1-9 (CCSS K.OA.4; NC Y1 number bonds to 10). The catalog topic is named 'Number bonds to 9' but the evidence is bonds to 10 — partners of 1 through 9. Teach bonds to 10.","Words to use: number bond, partner, ten-frame, make 10, pair, equation. Common mistakes: giving the same number again (7 needs 7); adding to a teen (7 + 7 = 14); sticking digits (5 + 5 read as 55); forgetting 5 + 5 and 10 + 0.","In the lab your child fills a ten-frame, counts empty spaces, matches partners, completes equations and snaps quick bonds."]},"familyTip":"Play 'I say 7, you say…' and wait for 3. Use fingers: hold up 4 and ask how many more fingers make 10. A muffin tin with 10 cups works as a giant ten-frame.","keyIdea":"Number bonds to 10 are pairs of numbers that add to exactly 10: 1+9, 2+8, 3+7, 4+6, 5+5 and the same pairs swapped. If 7 counters fill a ten-frame, 3 spaces stay empty, so 7 and 3 are partners. Knowing these pairs lets you make 10 quickly without counting every time.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Makes 10 or not? Drag each pair, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Makes 10","Does not make 10"],"items":[["7 + 3",0],["4 + 4",1],["5 + 5",0],["9 + 2",1],["1 + 9",0],["8 + 2",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagnbonds","caption":"Flagship lab: fill a ten-frame, count empty spaces, match partners to 10, complete equations and snap quick bonds.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_ghF3Vv6taM":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"4 + 0 =","choices":["4","40","0"],"answer":0,"hint":"Adding zero leaves the number the same.","explain":"4 + 0 = 4. Sticking digits would wrongly make 40."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"What does fluent within 5 mean?","choices":["knowing the answer straight away","always counting every finger","only using numbers bigger than 10"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fluent means quick and sure, not slow counting each time.","explain":"Fluent within 5 is instant recall of those small facts, not counting from 1 every time."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"5 − 0 =","choices":["5","0","50"],"answer":0,"hint":"Taking away zero leaves the start number.","explain":"5 − 0 = 5. You still have all five."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"3 + 3 = 6. Is this within 5?","choices":["no, the total is past 5","yes, because it uses 3","yes, because 3 is small"],"answer":0,"hint":"Check the total, not only the parts.","explain":"3 + 3 = 6, which is past 5, so it is outside the within-5 set."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"{n} bears show out of 5. How many hide?","answer":"5-n","hint":"Hidden means 5 take away the ones you can see.","explain":"5 − {n} = {=5-n} bears hide.","params":{"n":[0,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"5 − {n} =","answer":"5-n","hint":"Start at 5. Take away {n}. What number is left?","explain":"Start at 5 and remove {n}. You get {=5-n}. That is a take-away fact within 5.","params":{"n":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Partner of 1 to make 5?","choices":["4","1","5"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do you add to 1 so the total is exactly 5?","explain":"1 + 4 = 5, so the partner is 4."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"You see 2 dots and 2 dots. Total?","choices":["4","22","0"],"answer":0,"hint":"Subitise each group, then put them together.","explain":"2 + 2 = 4. Do not stick the digits to make 22."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"2 + 3 =","choices":["5","23","1"],"answer":0,"hint":"Put the two groups together. Do not stick the digits side by side.","explain":"2 and 3 make 5. 23 would be sticking digits, not adding."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"5 − 2 =","choices":["3","7","2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Start at 5 and take 2 away. What is left?","explain":"5 − 2 = 3. Adding would give 7; repeating 2 is not taking away."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"{n} + {k} =","answer":"n+k","hint":"Add the two numbers. Both are small, so you can say the total straight away.","explain":"{n} + {k} = {=n+k}. That is a fluent fact within 5.","params":{"n":[0,2],"k":[1,3]},"where":"n+k<=5"},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A five-frame has {n} counters. How many spaces are empty?","answer":"5-n","hint":"A five-frame has 5 spaces. Empty means 5 minus the full ones.","explain":"5 − {n} = {=5-n} empty spaces.","params":{"n":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which fact is within 5?","choices":["1 + 4","3 + 3","2 + 5"],"answer":0,"hint":"Within 5 means the total (or the start for a take-away) stays at 5 or less.","explain":"1 + 4 = 5, so it is within 5. 3 + 3 = 6 and 2 + 5 = 7 go past 5."}],"explain":{"kid":["Fluent means fast and sure. For numbers up to 5, you can say the answer straight away.","2 + 3 = 5. You do not need to count 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 on your fingers every time. You just know it.","Take-aways too: 5 − 2 = 3. Start with 5, remove 2, 3 are left.","A five-frame has 5 spaces. If 4 are full, 1 is empty. That shows 4 + 1 = 5 and 5 − 4 = 1."],"adult":["CCSS K.OA.5 asks children to fluently add and subtract within 5. NC Year 1 builds related facts within 10; within 5 is the first fluent set. Words to use: fluent, fact, total, take away, five-frame, subitise, partner, within 5.","Common mistakes: sticking digits (2 + 3 read as 23); always counting on fingers instead of recalling; treating 3 + 3 = 6 as a within-5 fact; swapping the sign (answering 5 − 2 as 7); echoing a number they can see instead of the hidden part.","In the lab your child flashes dots to subitise a sum, fills a five-frame, finds hidden bears with 5 − shown, races a slow finger count, builds every pair for 4, and picks the fact that matches a target."]},"familyTip":"Flash three fingers behind your back, then show them for one second. Ask 'how many?' before counting starts. Swap to 'I show 2, how many more to make 5?'","keyIdea":"Fluent within 5 means you know every add and take-away with numbers 0 to 5 straight away — 2 + 3 = 5, 5 − 2 = 3, 4 + 0 = 4 — without counting each finger every time. Subitising (seeing a small group at a glance) and a five-frame help. Facts past 5, like 3 + 3 = 6, are outside this set.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Within 5 or past 5? Drag each fact, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Within 5","Past 5"],"items":[["2 + 3",0],["3 + 3",1],["5 − 1",0],["4 + 4",1],["0 + 5",0],["2 + 4",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsum5","caption":"Flagship lab: flash dots, fill a five-frame, find hidden bears, race fingers, build every pair for 4, and pick the matching fact.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_ehGS_uVSJv":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"□ + 9 = 14 means □ is…","choices":["14 − 9 = 5","14 + 9 = 23","9 − 14"],"answer":0,"hint":"Use inverse subtract: total minus 9.","explain":"□ = 14 − 9 = 5. The inverse turns a missing addend into a subtract."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"14 − 9 = 5 undoes…","choices":["5 + 9 = 14","14 + 9 = 23","9 − 5 = 4"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the matching addition that these numbers undo.","explain":"14 − 9 = 5 undoes 5 + 9 = 14 — an undoing pair / fact family."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Knowing 13 + 5 = 18, 18 − 5 is…","choices":["13 — use the inverse","must recount from scratch","always 18 again"],"answer":0,"hint":"Reuse the known fact with the reverse operation.","explain":"Inverse gives 18 − 5 = 13 without recounting from scratch."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Addition and subtraction…","choices":["undo each other","never help each other","only work with the same sign"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think “undo” — one reverses the other.","explain":"Addition and subtraction undo each other, which is why checks and missing numbers work."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Check: {a} + {b} = {=a+b}. What is {=a+b} − {a}?","answer":"b","hint":"Subtract the other addend from the total to finish the check.","explain":"{=a+b} − {a} = {b}, so the original sum checks out both ways.","params":{"a":[5,12],"b":[3,7]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"□ + {b} = {t}. What is □? (Use {t} − {b}.)","answer":"t-b","hint":"Missing addend: take the known addend {b} from the total {t}.","explain":"□ = {t} − {b} = {=t-b}. Inverse solves the box.","params":{"t":[10,16],"b":[3,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Why learn inverse?","choices":["to check sums and find missing numbers","so you never subtract","because add and subtract are the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about checking a sum and finding a missing number.","explain":"Inverse checks calculations and finds missing numbers without starting over."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Saying add and subtract are unrelated is…","choices":["a mistake — they undo each other","always true","required"],"answer":0,"hint":"They form undoing pairs, not separate worlds.","explain":"Saying they are unrelated is a mistake — add and subtract undo each other."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"After 5 + 3 = 8, what undoes the add?","choices":["subtract 3","add 3 again","ignore the 3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the reverse of adding the same number.","explain":"Subtract 3 undoes add 3, so you return to 5. That is what inverse means."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"To check 15 + 7 = 22 you…","choices":["calculate 22 − 7 = 15","add 7 again","count only the 15"],"answer":0,"hint":"Use the reverse operation on the total to check the sum.","explain":"22 − 7 = 15 checks that 15 + 7 = 22 using the same three numbers."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"You know {a} + {b} = {=a+b}. What is {=a+b} − {b}?","answer":"a","hint":"Subtract undoes the add — take {b} from the total.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b}, so {=a+b} − {b} returns {a}. Inverse reuses the fact.","params":{"a":[4,9],"b":[2,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"□ + {b} = {=a+b}. What number goes in the box?","answer":"a","hint":"Use inverse: total minus the known addend {b}.","explain":"□ = {=a+b} − {b} = {a}. Missing-number problems use the undoing subtract.","params":{"a":[3,8],"b":[2,5]}},{"type":"order","id":"p5","prompt":"Order the steps to check a sum with inverse.","items":["write the addition fact","subtract the same addend from the total","see if you get the other addend"],"hint":"Start from the addition fact you already wrote, then reverse it.","explain":"Write the add, subtract the same addend from the total, and match the other addend."}],"explain":{"kid":["Add and take away undo each other.","If 5 + 3 makes 8, then 8 − 3 brings you back to 5.","You can check a sum by subtracting. You can find a missing number the same way.","You do not need to count all over again from the start."],"adult":["NC Y2 / CCSS 1.OA.4: use the inverse link between addition and subtraction to check calculations and solve missing-number problems. Words: inverse, undo, check, missing number, fact family.","Common mistakes: thinking add and subtract are unrelated; recounting from scratch instead of reusing a fact; for □ + 9 = 14 adding 14 + 9 instead of subtracting.","In the lab your child undoes an add with subtract, checks 15 + 7 = 22 using 22 − 7, solves □ + 9 = 14, shows a fact-family pair, and rejects the recount myth."]},"familyTip":"Write 13 + 5 = 18, then ask your child to say 18 − 5 = 13 without recounting. Next try □ + 9 = 14 using 14 − 9.","keyIdea":"Addition and subtraction undo each other. You can check a sum by subtracting, and solve missing-number problems with the inverse.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Inverse add and subtract — fair idea or myth? Drag each clue, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Fair idea","Myth / mix-up"],"items":[["addition undoes subtraction",0],["check 15 + 7 = 22 with 22 − 7 = 15",0],["□ + 9 = 14 means □ = 14 − 9",0],["add and subtract are unrelated",1],["you must always recount from scratch",1],["5 + 9 = 14 and 14 − 9 = 5 are a pair",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaginvadd","caption":"Flagship lab: undo an add, check a sum, solve a missing number, show a fact-family pair, reject recount-from-scratch, then challenge.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_QaYfeVL-0C":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Estimate 28+34 by rounding…","choices":["60","62","50"],"answer":0,"hint":"Round 28 to 30 and 34 to 30, then add.","explain":"30+30=60 is the estimate; 62 is exact."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Estimate 60 vs exact 62 means the estimate is…","choices":["a bit low","a bit high","useless"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare 60 with 62: which is smaller?","explain":"The estimate sits a little under the exact sum."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Ones digit 5 means…","choices":["round up","always round down","leave it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Use the midpoint rule when ones digit is 5.","explain":"5–9 round up to the next ten."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"An estimate…","choices":["is close, not always exact","must equal the exact answer","always makes numbers smaller"],"answer":0,"hint":"An estimate is a sensible near answer only.","explain":"Estimates are for speed and sense-checks."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"What is {n} rounded to the nearest ten?","answer":"20","hint":"Ones digit 0–4 means round down to the ten below.","explain":"{n} is closer to 20 than to 30, so it rounds to 20.","params":{"n":[16,19]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Estimate {a} + {b} (round each to 10, then add).","answer":"50","hint":"Round each addend first, then add the rounded values.","explain":"{a} rounds to 20 and {b} rounds to 30, so the estimate is 50.","params":{"a":[16,19],"b":[26,29]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Why estimate?","choices":["for a quick sensible check","to avoid all maths","because exact is wrong"],"answer":0,"hint":"Estimates give speed and a sense-check.","explain":"Estimates give a fast check before or instead of exact work."},{"type":"order","id":"m8","prompt":"Order: round 25 to the nearest 10.","items":["look at the ones digit (5)","apply round-up at 5","write 30"],"hint":"The ones digit decides up or down.","explain":"Ones digit 5 means round up, so 25 becomes 30."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"27 to the nearest 10 is…","choices":["30","20","27"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which multiple of ten sits closer to 27?","explain":"27 is closer to 30 than to 20."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"25 to the nearest 10 is…","choices":["30","20","15"],"answer":0,"hint":"Ones digit 5 means you round up to the next ten.","explain":"At the midpoint, round up to 30."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Round {n} to the nearest 10.","answer":"30","hint":"Look at the ones digit — 6 to 9 means round up.","explain":"{n} is closer to 30 than to 20, so it rounds to 30.","params":{"n":[26,29]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Estimate {a} + {b} by rounding each to the nearest 10, then adding.","answer":"60","hint":"Round both addends to a ten, then add those tens.","explain":"{a} rounds to 30 and {b} rounds to 30, so the estimate is 60.","params":{"a":[26,28],"b":[32,34]}},{"type":"order","id":"p5","prompt":"Order steps to estimate a sum.","items":["round each addend to the nearest 10","add the rounded numbers","compare with the exact sum if you need to"],"hint":"Round each addend before you add them.","explain":"Round, add for an estimate, then optionally check against exact."}],"explain":{"kid":["Rounding finds a nearby ten that is easy to use.","If the ones digit is 5 or more, round up. If it is 4 or less, round down.","To estimate a sum, round each number, then add.","An estimate is close and quick. It does not have to match the exact answer."],"adult":["NC Y3 / CCSS 3.NBT.A.1: round whole numbers to the nearest 10; use rounding to estimate sums. Words: round, nearest ten, estimate, midpoint, ones digit.","Common mistakes: always rounding down; thinking the estimate must equal the exact sum; leaving 5 as-is instead of rounding up; rounding only one addend.","In the lab your child rounds 27→30 and 25→30, estimates 28+34 as 60, sees that 60 is a bit low versus 62, and states the 5-up rule."]},"familyTip":"At the shop, round prices to the nearest 10p or £1 and add. Compare with the till total: was your estimate a bit low or high?","keyIdea":"Round each number to a nearby ten, then calculate. The result is an estimate — close and quick, not always exact.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Rounding & estimates — fair idea or myth? Drag each clue, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Fair idea","Myth / mix-up"],"items":[["27 rounds to 30",0],["25 rounds up to 30",0],["estimate 28+34 ≈ 60",0],["an estimate must equal the exact answer",1],["always round down to be safe",1],["ones digit 5 means round up",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagestm","caption":"Flagship lab: round on a number line, midpoint-5 rule, estimate a sum, judge low vs high, reject always-round-down, then challenge.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_s2mfRBoTal":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"9 + 6 equals…","choices":["15","16","14"],"answer":0,"hint":"Snap the bond total without counting every unit.","explain":"9 and 6 are partners to 15."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"11 + 7 equals…","choices":["18","17","19"],"answer":0,"hint":"Add eleven and seven for a within-20 bond.","explain":"11 + 7 = 18 is a number bond within 20."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which is a mix-up?","choices":["Bonds only ever make 10","Use 8+7 to get 15−7","Snap 9+6 as 15"],"answer":0,"hint":"Bonds within 20 cover many totals, not only ten.","explain":"Make-10 pairs are useful, but bonds within 20 include more totals."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Number bonds within 20 means…","choices":["pairs and related facts to 20","only ten-frame partners of 10","always count every object"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think pairs plus matching subtraction facts.","explain":"Recall addition pairs and related subtraction within 20."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Bond total: {a} + {b}","answer":"a+b","hint":"Sum the two parts of the bond.","explain":"The bond total is {=a+b}.","params":{"a":[4,9],"b":[5,8]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Related: ({a} + {b}) − {b} = ?","answer":"a","hint":"The leftover part equals the other addend.","explain":"Taking {b} from {a} + {b} leaves {=a}.","params":{"a":[6,9],"b":[4,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"13 − 5 using 8 + 5…","choices":["8","5","13"],"answer":0,"hint":"Flip the known addition fact to subtraction.","explain":"8 + 5 = 13, so 13 − 5 = 8."},{"type":"order","id":"m8","prompt":"Order: teach a within-20 bond.","items":["build the addition pair","name the related subtraction","practise quick recall"],"hint":"Build, relate, then snap.","explain":"Form the pair, link the take-away fact, then speed up recall."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"8 + 7 equals…","choices":["15","14","17"],"answer":0,"hint":"Add the two parts of the bond carefully.","explain":"8 and 7 are a bond to 15: 8 + 7 = 15."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"15 − 7 using the bond is…","choices":["8","7","15"],"answer":0,"hint":"Use the related addition fact 8 + 7 = 15.","explain":"Because 8 + 7 = 15, the related fact is 15 − 7 = 8."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"What is {a} + {b}?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the two parts to find the bond total.","explain":"Add the parts: {a} + {b} makes the bond total {=a+b}.","params":{"a":[6,9],"b":[4,8]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Related fact: ({a} + {b}) − {b} = ?","answer":"a","hint":"Related subtraction returns the other part of the bond.","explain":"From {a} + {b}, taking away {b} leaves {=a}.","params":{"a":[5,9],"b":[3,7]}},{"type":"order","id":"p5","prompt":"Order: use a bond for subtraction.","items":["recall the addition pair","write the related take-away","check the leftover part"],"hint":"Know the sum first, then flip to subtraction.","explain":"Recall a + b = c, then use c − b = a, then check."}],"explain":{"kid":["A number bond is a pair that adds to a total. Within 20 you need many pairs, not only partners of 10.","If 8 + 7 = 15, then 15 − 7 = 8. The same bond helps subtraction.","Try to snap the answer quickly: hear 9 + 6 and say 15 without counting every bead.","Bonds within 20 are addition pairs and the matching take-away facts you can recall."],"adult":["NC Y1 addition and subtraction: recall number bonds (addition and related subtraction facts) within 20. Distinct from bonds to 10 on a ten-frame (K.OA.4 / 'Number bonds to 9' topic).","Words: number bond, pair, related fact, within 20, recall. Common mistakes: treating bonds as only make-10; recounting every subtraction from 1; off-by-one sums (8+7 as 14 or 17).","In the lab your child builds 8+7 and 11+7, uses 15−7=8, snaps 9+6, and spots the make-10-only mix-up."]},"familyTip":"Play 'I say 8 + 7' and wait for 15, then ask 'so 15 take away 7?'. Use two colours of counters for each part of the bond.","keyIdea":"Number bonds within 20 are pairs of numbers that add to a total up to 20, plus the related subtraction fact. If 8 + 7 = 15, then 15 − 7 = 8. Quick recall of these bonds means you do not recount from one every time.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"True bond idea or mix-up? Drag each clue, or tap it then a box.","opts":{"bins":["True bond idea","Myth / mix-up"],"items":[["8 + 7 = 15 is a bond within 20",0],["15 − 7 = 8 uses the related fact",0],["bonds only ever make exactly 10",1],["9 + 6 snaps to 15",0],["always recount every subtraction from 1",1],["pairs and related facts up to 20",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagnb20","caption":"Flagship lab: build bonds to 15 and 18, use related subtraction, snap recall, catch make-10-only myth.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_PgsHGYJMH-":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"3 fingers and 2 fingers show…","choices":["5","32","1"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count all raised fingers together.","explain":"Three and two fingers represent a total of five."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"5 take away 2 leaves…","choices":["3","5","2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Remove two objects from five, then count.","explain":"Five take away two leaves three."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which is a mix-up?","choices":["Only write digits, never show","Use cubes for 3 + 2","Draw the take-away"],"answer":0,"hint":"K.OA.1 wants more than written numerals alone.","explain":"Representations include objects, drawings, and fingers."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Representing + and − means…","choices":["objects, drawings, fingers","only writing 3+2=5","never using fingers"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of concrete and mental models.","explain":"Show operations with objects, drawings, and fingers."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Objects: {a} + {b} altogether","answer":"a+b","hint":"Join both groups of objects.","explain":"Altogether there are {=a+b} objects.","params":{"a":[1,6],"b":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Take-away: {a} − {b} left","answer":"a-b","hint":"Remove b from a, then count remaining.","explain":"After taking {b} from {a}, {=a-b} remain.","params":{"a":[5,10],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A finger model of 4 − 1…","choices":["3 left up","must write only","needs 41 fingers"],"answer":0,"hint":"Start with four fingers, fold one down.","explain":"Four take away one leaves three fingers up."},{"type":"order","id":"m8","prompt":"Order: represent a take-away.","items":["start with the whole set","remove the taken objects","count what is left"],"hint":"Whole, remove, then leftover.","explain":"Show the start amount, take some away, name the remainder."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"3 objects and 2 more altogether…","choices":["5","3","2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Join both object groups, then count them all.","explain":"Three objects plus two more make five altogether."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"A drawing of 4 + 1 needs…","choices":["5 apple marks","only the digit 5","no pictures"],"answer":0,"hint":"Sketch one mark for each apple in the story.","explain":"Four plus one is shown by five marks in the drawing."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"{a} objects join {b} more. Altogether?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Combine the two groups of objects.","explain":"{a} and {b} objects altogether make {=a+b}.","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Start with {a}, take away {b}. Left?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Remove b objects, then count what remains.","explain":"{a} take away {b} leaves {=a-b}.","params":{"a":[4,9],"b":[1,3]}},{"type":"order","id":"p5","prompt":"Order: represent an addition story.","items":["show with objects or fingers","say how many altogether","write the number sentence if ready"],"hint":"Concrete model first, then the spoken total.","explain":"Represent, name the total, then optionally write symbols."}],"explain":{"kid":["You can show adding with objects. Put 3 here and 2 there, then join them.","You can also draw the story, or put up fingers. That still shows the same sum.","For take-away, start with objects and remove some. Count what is left.","Writing 3 + 2 = 5 is fine later — first show it with things, pictures, or fingers."],"adult":["CCSS K.OA.1 / NC Y1: represent addition and subtraction with objects, fingers, mental images, drawings, sounds, acting out, verbal explanations, expressions, or equations.","Words: represent, objects, drawing, fingers, altogether, take away. Common mistakes: only writing numerals; thinking fingers are cheating; skipping a concrete model for subtraction.","In the lab your child joins 3+2 objects, draws 4+1, shows fingers, takes 2 from 5, and spots the digits-only mix-up."]},"familyTip":"Ask your child to show 4 + 1 three ways: with counters, a quick drawing, and fingers. Then try 5 − 2 the same three ways.","keyIdea":"Addition and subtraction can be represented with objects, drawings, and mental images such as fingers — not only with written number sentences. Showing 3 + 2 with cubes, a sketch, or raised fingers is the same sum.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Fair representation or mix-up? Drag each clue, or tap it then a box.","opts":{"bins":["Fair representation","Myth / mix-up"],"items":[["use cubes for 3 + 2",0],["draw the take-away story",0],["only write digits, never show",1],["raise fingers for a sum",0],["fingers never count as a model",1],["objects, drawings, and fingers",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagrepass","caption":"Flagship lab: objects for +/−, draw the story, fingers, catch digits-only myth.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt__we2TDqnJx":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"7 + 3 equals…","choices":["10","73","4"],"answer":0,"hint":"Join seven and three. Do not stick the digits.","explain":"7 + 3 = 10. Sticking digits would wrongly make 73."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which is a within-10 fact?","choices":["5 + 3 = 8","5 + 6 = 11","12 − 1 = 11"],"answer":0,"hint":"Totals and starts stay from 0 to 10.","explain":"5 + 3 = 8 stays within 10. The others go past 10."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which is the mix-up?","choices":["Within 10 means only make-10 pairs","Use 6+4 to get 10−4","Snap 7+3 as 10"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about facts like 5 + 3 as well as 7 + 3.","explain":"Within 10 includes every fact with numbers 0–10, not only make-10."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Fluent within 10 means…","choices":["knowing facts 0–10 straight away","always counting every counter","only adding, never taking away"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fluent is quick recall of adds and take-aways.","explain":"You know the fact without recounting; take-aways count too."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Ten-frame: {a} filled. Empty?","answer":"10-a","hint":"Empty spaces are 10 take away the filled count.","explain":"{a} filled leaves {=10-a} empty on a ten-frame.","params":{"a":[3,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"If a number + {b} = 10, then 10 − {b} equals that number. What is 10 − {b}?","answer":"10-b","hint":"The related take-away undoes the add to 10.","explain":"If ? + {b} = 10, then 10 − {b} = {=10-b}.","params":{"b":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"9 − 2 equals…","choices":["7","11","92"],"answer":0,"hint":"Start at nine and take away two. Do not stick digits or add.","explain":"9 − 2 = 7. Do not add, and do not stick digits."},{"type":"order","id":"m8","prompt":"Order: use a related take-away.","items":["know the addition fact","keep the same three numbers","write the take-away"],"hint":"Start from the known add, then flip to subtraction.","explain":"Related facts reuse the same three numbers with a take-away."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"8 on a ten-frame. Empty spaces?","choices":["2","8","10"],"answer":0,"hint":"A ten-frame has ten spaces. Count how many are empty.","explain":"8 filled leaves 2 empty because 8 + 2 = 10."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"10 − 4 using 6 + 4 is…","choices":["6","4","10"],"answer":0,"hint":"Use the related addition fact with the same three numbers.","explain":"Because 6 + 4 = 10, the related fact is 10 − 4 = 6."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"What is {a} + {b}?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the two parts. Stay with totals of 10 or less.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b}. That is a within-10 fact.","params":{"a":[2,7],"b":[1,3]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"What is {a} − {b}?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Start at a and take away b.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b}. Use a related add if it helps.","params":{"a":[6,10],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"order","id":"p5","prompt":"Order: make-10 bridge for 8 + 2.","items":["see 8 needs 2 to make 10","add the 2 to fill the frame","say the total 10"],"hint":"Spot the gap to 10, fill it, name the total.","explain":"Bridge through 10: find how many to 10, add that part, name 10."}],"explain":{"kid":["Fluent within 10 means you know the answer straight away for adds and take-aways with numbers from 0 to 10.","A ten-frame shows how many are filled and how many are empty. Make 10: 8 needs 2 more.","If 6 + 4 = 10, then 10 − 4 = 6. The same three numbers make a related fact.","Facts within 10 are not only pairs that make 10. 5 + 3 = 8 counts too."],"adult":["CCSS 1.OA.6: fluently add and subtract within 10. NC Y1/Y2: related number bonds and facts within 10. Distinct from within-5 fluency (flagsum5) and from partners that make exactly 10 on a ten-frame (flagnbonds).","Words: ten-frame, make-10 bridge, related fact, fluent, within 10. Common mistakes: sticking digits (7+3 as 73); treating within 10 as only make-10 pairs; recounting every counter instead of snapping the fact.","In the lab your child fills 8 on a ten-frame, bridges 8+2 through 10, uses 10−4=6, snaps 7+3, and spots the make-10-only mix-up."]},"familyTip":"Flash a ten-frame with 8 filled and ask how many empty. Then say '7 + 3' and wait for a snap '10'. Follow with 'so 10 take away 3?'.","keyIdea":"Fluent within 10 means you know every addition and subtraction fact with numbers 0–10 straight away — ten-frame pictures, make-10 bridges, and related take-aways — without recounting every counter.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Within-10 fluency idea or mix-up? Drag each clue, or tap it then a box.","opts":{"bins":["True fluency idea","Myth / mix-up"],"items":[["7 + 3 = 10 is a within-10 fact",0],["10 − 4 = 6 uses a related fact",0],["fluent within 10 means only make-10 pairs",1],["5 + 3 = 8 stays within 10",0],["stick 7 and 3 together as 73",1],["snap the answer without recounting",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsum10","caption":"Flagship lab: ten-frame fill, make-10 bridge, related take-away, snap recall, near-10 facts, catch make-10-only myth.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_m1W6nTQJ2b":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"9 + 7 equals…","choices":["16","97","2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Make ten from 9, then add the rest of 7.","explain":"9 + 1 = 10, then + 6 → 16."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"16 − 7 using 9 + 7 is…","choices":["9","7","16"],"answer":0,"hint":"Related facts share the same three numbers.","explain":"If 9 + 7 = 16, then 16 − 7 = 9."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which is a within-20 make-ten step?","choices":["8 + 2 = 10, then + 4","stop at 10 and ignore the rest","write 8 + 6 as 86"],"answer":0,"hint":"Make ten is a bridge, then you add what is left.","explain":"Fill to 10, then add the leftover part."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"12 − 4 via a ten equals…","choices":["8","16","2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Take 2 to reach 10, then take 2 more.","explain":"12 − 2 = 10, then − 2 = 8."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which is the mix-up?","choices":["8 + 6 via make-ten is written 86","Make-ten can build facts past 10","Count on from the larger addend"],"answer":0,"hint":"Spot the stuck-digits error.","explain":"Make-ten gives 14, not the stuck digits 86."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"{a} − {b} (break to a ten). Answer?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Hit ten first, then finish the take-away.","explain":"Break {a} − {b} by hitting ten first, then finish to reach {=a-b}.","params":{"a":[11,15],"b":[3,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Count-on for 9 + 3 starts…","choices":["at 9, then 10, 11, 12","by recounting every object from 1","by writing 93"],"answer":0,"hint":"Keep the larger number; count the smaller.","explain":"Count on from 9 three times to reach 12."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Within 20 strategies include…","choices":["make ten, decompose to a ten, count on","only partners that make exactly 10","only sticking digits together"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of bridges through ten and counting on.","explain":"Make ten, decompose leading to a ten, and count on are core within-20 strategies."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"Make-ten: {a} + {b}. Total?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Fill {a} up to 10 using part of {b}, then add the leftover.","explain":"Make ten from {a}, then add the leftover of {b} to reach {=a+b}.","params":{"a":[7,9],"b":[4,7]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"8 + 6 via make-ten equals…","choices":["14","86","10"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fill 8 up to 10 with 2 from the 6, then add the leftover 4.","explain":"8 + 2 = 10, then + 4 → 14. Do not stick digits as 86."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"13 − 5 by hitting ten first equals…","choices":["8","15","3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Take 3 to reach 10, then take 2 more.","explain":"13 − 3 = 10, then − 2 = 8."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Best first move for 9 + 4?","choices":["Start at 9 and count 4 more","Recount every object from 1","Write 94"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count on from the larger addend.","explain":"Count on: 10, 11, 12, 13. Do not stick digits or restart from 1."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Make-ten: {a} needs how many more to make 10?","answer":"10-a","hint":"Spaces left on a ten-frame that already shows {a}.","explain":"10 − {a} = {=10-a} more makes ten.","params":{"a":[6,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"{a} + {b} (make ten, then add). Total?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Fill {a} up to 10, then add what is left of {b}.","explain":"Make ten from {a}, then add the leftover of {b} to reach {=a+b}.","params":{"a":[7,9],"b":[3,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Within 20 means sums and take-aways that stay between 0 and 20.","Make ten: for 8 + 6, take 2 from the 6 to fill 8 up to 10, then add the leftover 4 to get 14.","For take-away, break the number so you hit ten first — 13 − 5 becomes 13 − 3 = 10, then − 2 = 8.","You can also count on from the larger number, or use a related fact like 9 + 7 = 16 so 16 − 7 = 9."],"adult":["CCSS 1.OA.6: add and subtract within 20, demonstrating fluency within 10; use strategies such as counting on, making ten (e.g. 8 + 6 = 8 + 2 + 4), and decomposing a number leading to a ten (e.g. 13 − 4 = 13 − 3 − 1). Distinct from Fluent adding and subtracting within 10 (snap facts 0–10) and Number bonds within 20 (exact partner pairs).","Words: make ten, decompose, count on, related fact, within 20. Common mistakes: sticking digits (8 + 6 as 86); treating make-ten as stopping at 10; recounting every object from 1 instead of counting on.","In the lab your child fills a ten-frame toward 10, shows the make-ten bridge for 8 + 6, breaks 13 − 5 to a ten, uses a related take-away, picks count-on over recount, and spots the stick-digits mix-up."]},"familyTip":"Ask: for 8 + 6, how many more make 8 into 10? Take those 2 from the 6, then add the leftover 4. Try the same break-to-ten idea with 13 − 5.","keyIdea":"Within 20 you use strategies — make ten then add the rest, decompose a take-away so you hit ten first, count on from the larger number, and use related facts — not only snap recall of facts within 10.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Within-20 strategy or mix-up? Drag each clue, or tap it then a box.","opts":{"bins":["True strategy idea","Myth / mix-up"],"items":[["make ten then add the leftover",0],["decompose a take-away to hit ten first",0],["write 8 + 6 as the stuck digits 86",1],["count on from the larger addend",0],["related fact: 9 + 7 helps 16 − 7",0],["within 20 means only partners that make 10",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagw20","caption":"Flagship lab: fill toward ten, make-ten bridge for 8+6, decompose 13−5 to a ten, related take-away, count-on vs recount, catch stick-digits myth.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt__t4afSyZRm":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"9 + 6 equals…","choices":["15","96","3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Snap the one-digit sum. Do not stick the digits together.","explain":"9 + 6 = 15. Sticking digits would wrongly make ninety-six."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Fluent within 20 means…","choices":["knowing one-digit sums from memory","always counting every counter","only doubles, never near-doubles"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fluency is quick known facts, not endless recounting.","explain":"By the end of this stage you know every sum of two one-digit numbers from memory."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"What is {=a+a} − {a}?","answer":"a","hint":"A double take-away one of the parts leaves the other part.","explain":"{=a+a} − {a} = {a}. Related fact from the double.","params":{"a":[6,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which is a within-20 one-digit sum?","choices":["9 + 8 = 17","9 + 12 = 21","15 + 6 = 21"],"answer":0,"hint":"Both addends should be one-digit (0–9).","explain":"9 and 8 are one-digit; 17 is within 20. The others use a two-digit addend or go past."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A mix-up says 9 + 6 = 96. What went wrong?","choices":["Digits were stuck together","The double was used","Related subtraction was used"],"answer":0,"hint":"Joining digit glyphs is not adding their values.","explain":"Sticking 9 and 6 makes ninety-six. Adding values makes fifteen."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Double {a}, then add 1. What total?","answer":"a+a+1","hint":"Near-double: twice the number, plus one.","explain":"Double {a} is {=a+a}. Plus 1 is {=a+a+1}.","params":{"a":[5,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"15 − 8 using 7 + 8 is…","choices":["7","8","23"],"answer":0,"hint":"Same three numbers: 7, 8 and 15.","explain":"If 7 + 8 = 15, then 15 − 8 = 7."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Why are doubles useful within 20?","choices":["They are known anchors for near-doubles","They only work under 10","They replace all subtraction"],"answer":0,"hint":"Near-doubles sit one away from a known double.","explain":"Knowing 7+7 unlocks 7+8. Doubles are memory anchors, not the only facts."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"8 + 8 equals…","choices":["16","88","0"],"answer":0,"hint":"A double is a number added to itself. Twice eight.","explain":"8 + 8 is the double of 8, which is 16. Not eighty-eight."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"7 + 8 using double 7 is…","choices":["15","78","1"],"answer":0,"hint":"Double 7 is 14. Near-double means add one more.","explain":"Double 7 is 14. 7 + 8 is one more than that double, so 15."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"What is {a} + {a}?","answer":"a+a","hint":"This is a double. The same number plus itself.","explain":"{a} + {a} = {=a+a}. Doubles are anchor facts within 20.","params":{"a":[6,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"16 − 7 using 9 + 7 is…","choices":["9","7","23"],"answer":0,"hint":"Related facts share the same three numbers: 9, 7 and 16.","explain":"If 9 + 7 = 16, then 16 − 7 = 9. Keep the same three numbers."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"Near-double: what is {a} + {=a+1}?","answer":"a+a+1","hint":"Double {a}, then add one for the near-double.","explain":"Double {a} is {=a+a}. Plus one more is {=a+a+1}.","params":{"a":[6,9]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Fluent within 20 means you know the answer straight away for sums like 8+8 or 9+6.","A double is a number plus itself: 6+6=12, 7+7=14, 8+8=16, 9+9=18. These are anchor facts.","A near-double is one more than a double. 7+8 is double 7, then plus 1, which is 15.","If you know 9+7=16, you also know 16−7=9. Related facts share the same three numbers."],"adult":["Ages 7 to 8. CCSS 2.OA.2: fluently add and subtract within 20 using mental strategies; by end of Grade 2, know from memory all sums of two one-digit numbers. Builds on addition and subtraction within 20 (strategies) and fluent within 10.","Doubles and near-doubles are the main memory anchors. Make-ten is still a strategy (taught in the within-20 strategies lesson); this lesson pushes the known-from-memory bar for one-digit + one-digit and related subtraction.","Common mistakes: sticking digits together (9+6 as 96); always recounting every counter; treating fluency as only make-10 pairs; and forgetting that related subtraction uses the same three numbers.","In the lab your child builds doubles, opens a near-double for 7+8, snaps 9+6, derives 16−7 from 9+7, and rejects the 'always recount' myth."]},"familyTip":"Fire a quick round: 8+8, 7+8, 9+6, 16−7. Celebrate snap answers. If your child counts every finger, pause and ask: what double is close?","keyIdea":"Fluent within 20 means you know from memory every sum of two one-digit numbers, and the related take-aways, using doubles and near-doubles as anchors — without recounting every counter each time.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Fluent fact or recount trap? Drag each into its box, or tap then a box.","opts":{"bins":["known fact","recount trap"],"items":[["8 + 8 = 16",0],["count 9+6 on every finger",1],["7 + 8 from double 7",0],["stick 9 and 6 as 96",1],["16 − 7 from 9+7",0],["always start from 1",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsum20","caption":"Flagship lab: doubles wall, near-doubles, snap recall, related take-aways, catch recount myth.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_ewmuMMPAzP":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"458 + 367 = ?","choices":["825","715","725"],"answer":0,"hint":"There are two swaps: one in the ones and one in the tens.","explain":"8 + 7 = 15 ones: write 5, carry 1. 5 + 6 + 1 = 12 tens: write 2, carry 1. 4 + 3 + 1 = 8 hundreds. The answer is 825."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"503 − 148 = ?","choices":["355","365","445"],"answer":0,"hint":"There are no tens. Break a hundred into 10 tens, then a ten into 10 ones.","explain":"503 becomes 4 hundreds, 9 tens and 13 ones. 13 − 8 = 5, 9 − 4 = 5, 4 − 1 = 3. The answer is 355."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"13 tens is the same as…","choices":["1 hundred and 3 tens","1 ten and 3 ones","13 hundreds"],"answer":0,"hint":"How many tens make 1 hundred?","explain":"10 tens make 1 hundred, so 13 tens is 1 hundred and 3 tens, which is 130."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Mia works out 352 − 127 and gets 235. What went wrong?","choices":["she did 7 − 2 instead of breaking a ten","she forgot to carry","nothing, 235 is right"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the ones: the top digit is 2.","explain":"She took the smaller digit from the bigger one. She should break a ten so the ones are 12, and 12 − 7 = 5. The answer is 225."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A school has {=a*10+6} pupils. {=b*10+8} more join. How many pupils are there now?","answer":"a*10+6+b*10+8","hint":"Add the ones, then the tens, then the hundreds, carrying when a column reaches 10.","explain":"6 + 8 = 14 ones: write 4 and carry 1 ten. Then add the tens and hundreds: {=a*10+6} + {=b*10+8} = {=a*10+b*10+14} pupils.","params":{"a":[23,54],"b":[17,36]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A book has {=a*100+3} pages. Sam has read {=b*10+7}. How many pages are left?","answer":"a*100+3-b*10-7","hint":"There are no tens in the top number. Break a hundred into 10 tens, then a ten into 10 ones.","explain":"{=a*100+3} has 0 tens, so break a hundred into 10 tens, then a ten into 10 ones. {=a*100+3} − {=b*10+7} = {=a*100-b*10-4} pages left.","params":{"a":[6,9],"b":[15,38]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"In 365 + 271, why is there a small 1 above the hundreds?","choices":["6 tens + 7 tens = 13 tens, and 10 tens make 1 hundred","6 + 7 = 13 ones","there is always a 1 there"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which column added up to 10 or more?","explain":"The tens made 13 tens. 10 of them were swapped for 1 hundred, which is the small 1. The answer is 636."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which sum needs no swapping at all?","choices":["321 + 456","247 + 136","458 + 367"],"answer":0,"hint":"Add each column. Does any reach 10?","explain":"1 + 6 = 7, 2 + 5 = 7 and 3 + 4 = 7, so 321 + 456 = 777 with no swaps. The other two have a column that reaches 10 or more."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Work out 247 + 136.","choices":["383","3713","373"],"answer":0,"hint":"7 ones + 6 ones = 13 ones. Swap 10 ones for 1 ten.","explain":"Ones: 13 is 1 ten and 3 ones, so write 3 and carry 1. Tens: 4 + 3 + 1 = 8. Hundreds: 2 + 1 = 3. The answer is 383."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Work out 352 − 127.","choices":["225","235","479"],"answer":0,"hint":"You cannot take 7 from 2. Break a ten into 10 ones first.","explain":"Break a ten: 4 tens and 12 ones. 12 − 7 = 5, 4 − 2 = 2, 3 − 1 = 2. The answer is 225. Swapping to 7 − 2 gives the wrong answer 235."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Add {=a*10+8} + {=b*10+5}.","answer":"a*10+8+b*10+5","hint":"Start with the ones. If a column reaches 10 or more, swap 10 for 1 in the next column.","explain":"Ones: 8 + 5 = 13, so write 3 and carry 1 ten. Then add the tens and the hundreds. {=a*10+8} + {=b*10+5} = {=a*10+b*10+13}.","params":{"a":[15,47],"b":[12,34]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Take away: {=a*10+2} − {=b*10+7}.","answer":"a*10+2-b*10-7","hint":"Start with the ones. If the top digit is too small, break 1 from the next column into 10.","explain":"2 − 7 will not go, so break a ten: 12 − 7 = 5. Then take away the tens and the hundreds. {=a*10+2} − {=b*10+7} = {=a*10-b*10-5}. Check by adding back.","params":{"a":[61,93],"b":[14,38]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"In 425 − 162, the tens are 2 − 6. What should you do?","choices":["break 1 hundred into 10 tens","work out 6 − 2","break 1 ten into 10 ones"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which column is next to the tens on the left?","explain":"Break 1 hundred into 10 tens: now 3 hundreds and 12 tens. 12 − 6 = 6 tens and 3 − 1 = 2 hundreds, so 425 − 162 = 263."}],"explain":{"kid":["A three-digit number is made of hundreds, tens and ones. 247 is 2 hundreds, 4 tens and 7 ones. To add or take away, line the numbers up in columns and start with the ones.","Each column can hold up to 9. In 247 + 136, the ones make 7 + 6 = 13. That is too many for one column. So swap 10 ones for 1 ten. Write 3 in the ones and a small 1 in the tens. This swap is called regrouping, or carrying.","Taking away works the other way. In 352 − 127, you cannot take 7 ones from 2 ones. Do not work out 7 − 2 instead. Break 1 ten into 10 ones. Now there are 12 ones and 4 tens. 12 − 7 = 5, then 4 − 2 = 2 and 3 − 1 = 2. The answer is 225.","Sometimes there are no tens to break, as in 503 − 148. Break a hundred into 10 tens first. Then break one of those tens into 10 ones. You get 4 hundreds, 9 tens and 13 ones."],"adult":["Ages 7 to 8. This matches CCSS 2.NBT.7 (add and subtract within 1000 using concrete models or drawings and strategies based on place value, composing or decomposing tens or hundreds when necessary) and Year 3 Number in the National Curriculum in England (add and subtract numbers with up to three digits, using formal written methods of columnar addition and subtraction). It builds on The three digits of a three-digit number.","The written column method works because of place value. Composing (regrouping or carrying) replaces 10 units of one place with 1 unit of the next place. Decomposing (exchanging or borrowing) does the reverse. The total value never changes, only how it is shown. Base-ten blocks make that visible before the small carried and renamed digits are written.","Common mistakes: writing 13 in the ones column, which gives 3713 for 247 + 136. Forgetting to add the carried digit. Subtracting the smaller digit from the larger whatever its place, so 352 − 127 becomes 235. Forgetting to reduce the column that was broken. And, with a zero, forgetting that 10 tens become 9 once a ten has gone to the ones, so 503 − 148 becomes 365 instead of 355.","In the lab your child adds by dragging 10 ones into the tens and 10 tens into the hundreds, including a sum that needs two swaps. They then take away, breaking a ten, then a hundred, then both across a zero. Each move updates the written column sum beside the blocks, so the small digits always match something they did."]},"familyTip":"Use coins: 1p, 10p and £1 (or cents, dimes and dollars). Pay for something costing 247 with 3 hundreds and see how the change needs a hundred broken into tens and a ten broken into ones. Then add two prices and swap ten 1p coins for a 10p.","keyIdea":"To add or subtract three-digit numbers, work one column at a time, starting with the ones. Each column can hold at most 9. When adding gives 10 or more in a column, swap 10 of them for 1 in the next column to the left: 10 ones make a ten and 10 tens make a hundred. When subtracting and a column is too small, break 1 from the next column to the left into 10. Never swap the digits round.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does this sum need a swap? Drag each card into a box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["needs a swap or a break","no swap needed"],"items":[["247 + 136",0],["321 + 456",1],["352 − 127",0],["586 − 234",1],["503 − 148",0],["612 + 175",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagregroup","caption":"Flagship lab: drag blocks to swap 10 ones for a ten or 10 tens for a hundred, break blocks to take away, and watch the written sum.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_glPPG-kTQY":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"Work out {a} + {b}. The answer goes past a ten.","answer":"a+b","hint":"How far is {a} from the next ten? Jump that far, then add the rest.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b}. First jump to the next ten, then add what is left.","params":{"a":[57,68],"b":[4,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"A jar has {a} beads. You add {t} bags of 10 beads. How many beads now?","answer":"a+10*t","hint":"Add {t} tens to the tens digit. The ones digit stays the same.","explain":"{a} + {=10*t} = {=a+10*t}. {t} bags of 10 is {t} tens, so the tens digit goes up by {t}.","params":{"a":[12,27],"t":[3,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"52 + 30: which digit changes?","choices":["only the tens digit","only the ones digit","both digits"],"answer":0,"hint":"30 is 3 tens. Does adding tens change the ones?","explain":"52 + 30 = 82. Adding 3 tens changes only the tens digit; the 2 ones stay."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which equation shows the jumps for 58 + 6?","choices":["58 + 2 + 4 = 60 + 4 = 64","58 + 3 + 3 = 61 + 3 = 64","58 + 6 = 514"],"answer":0,"hint":"58 needs 2 more to reach a whole ten.","explain":"58 + 2 = 60, a whole ten, then 60 + 4 = 64. The equation records both jumps."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Mo says 46 + 7 = 43. What is the slip?","choices":["the answer must go past 50, so it is 53","Mo should take away instead","nothing, 43 is right"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is 43 bigger or smaller than 46?","explain":"Adding makes the number bigger, so the answer cannot be 43. 46 + 4 = 50, then 3 more is 53."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"How far is it from 26 to the next ten?","choices":["4","6","14"],"answer":0,"hint":"The next ten after 26 is 30. What makes 10 with 6?","explain":"26 + 4 = 30, because 6 + 4 = 10."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which is the quickest way to work out 64 + 30?","choices":["add 3 to the tens digit: 94","count on 30 ones from 64","add 3 to the ones digit: 67"],"answer":0,"hint":"30 is 3 tens. Which digit of 64 shows the tens?","explain":"64 + 30 = 94. Three tens go onto the tens digit; counting 30 ones is slow and easy to get wrong."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"How far is it from {a} to the next ten?","answer":"10-a%10","hint":"Look at the ones digit of {a}. What makes 10 with it?","explain":"{a} + {=10-a%10} = {=a+10-a%10}, a whole ten.","params":{"a":[41,49]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"46 + 7: which split of 7 gets you to a whole ten first?","choices":["4 and 3, because 46 + 4 = 50","5 and 2, because 5 is half of 10","1 and 6, because you count on one"],"answer":0,"hint":"How many more does 46 need to make 50? Think 6 + ? = 10.","explain":"46 needs 4 to make 50, so split 7 into 4 and 3. Then 50 + 3 = 53."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Work out {a} + {b}.","answer":"a+b","hint":"Jump from {a} to the next ten first, then add what is left of {b}.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b}. Bridge to the next ten, then add the rest of the ones.","params":{"a":[36,48],"b":[5,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Work out {a} + {=10*t}.","answer":"a+10*t","hint":"{=10*t} is {t} tens. Add {t} to the tens digit of {a} and keep the ones.","explain":"{a} + {=10*t} = {=a+10*t}. Only the tens digit changes, because we added {t} tens.","params":{"a":[21,39],"t":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"How far is it from 82 to the next ten?","choices":["8","2","18"],"answer":0,"hint":"The next ten after 82 is 90. What makes 10 with 2?","explain":"82 + 8 = 90, because 2 + 8 = 10. The ones digit 2 is not the jump; its bond to 10 is."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Sam says 38 + 40 = 42. What went wrong?","choices":["Sam added 4 ones, but 40 is 4 tens","Sam should count on in ones","nothing, 42 is right"],"answer":0,"hint":"How many tens are in 40? Is 40 the same as 4?","explain":"40 is 4 tens, so add to the tens digit: 3 tens + 4 tens = 7 tens. 38 + 40 = 78."}],"explain":{"kid":["You do not need to count on in ones. Use tens and ones to add fast.","To work out 46 + 7, first jump to the next ten. 46 needs 4 more to make 50. Then add the 3 that are left. So 46 + 7 = 53. We split 7 into 4 and 3.","To work out 38 + 40, add tens. 40 is 4 tens. 3 tens and 4 tens make 7 tens. The 8 ones stay the same, so 38 + 40 = 78.","Number bonds to 10 help a lot. From 57, the next ten is 60, and the jump is 3, because 7 + 3 = 10."],"adult":["Ages 6 to 7. This is CCSS 1.NBT.4 (add within 100, including a two-digit number and a one-digit number, and a two-digit number and a multiple of 10, using place value strategies) and NC Year 2 addition: add a two-digit number and ones, and a two-digit number and tens. Key words: place value, tens, ones, partition, bridge through ten, number bond, multiple of ten.","Bridging through ten uses a known bond: for 46 + 7, the bond 6 + 4 = 10 says how far it is to 50. The 7 is then partitioned into 4 and 3. Adding a multiple of ten keeps the ones digit and raises the tens digit, like moving down a column of a hundred square. Recording the jumps as 46 + 7 = 46 + 4 + 3 = 50 + 3 = 53 links the mental strategy to written work.","Common mistakes: writing the digits side by side (46 + 7 = 413); staying in the same decade (46 + 7 = 43); adding a multiple of ten as ones (38 + 40 = 42); jumping by the ones digit instead of its bond to 10 (from 57, jumping 7, not 3); and counting on in ones, which is slow and error-prone.","In the lab your child drags a pair of scissors along a row of ones cubes to split them so the first jump lands on a whole ten. Next they drag ten rods into 38 and watch only the tens digit grow, while a '4 ones' card shows why 42 is wrong. Then they drag a dot to the next ten from 57, 82 and 26. They sort six sums by which digit changes, and last they fill the boxes in 58 + 6 = 58 + 2 + 4 = 60 + 4 = 64."]},"familyTip":"Play 'next ten' in the car or at the shops. Say a number like 37 and your child says how far to the next ten (3, to make 40). Then try a sum: 37 + 5. Ask them to say the two jumps out loud, 37 + 3 = 40, then 40 + 2 = 42.","keyIdea":"Use place value to add within 100 without counting in ones. To add ones, bridge through the next ten: 46 + 7 = 46 + 4 + 3 = 50 + 3 = 53, using the number bond 6 + 4 = 10. To add a multiple of ten, add to the tens digit only: 38 + 40 = 78, because 3 tens + 4 tens = 7 tens and the 8 ones stay the same.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which digit changes? Drag each sum, or tap it then a box.","opts":{"bins":["only the tens digit","both digits"],"items":[["24 + 50 = 74",0],["36 + 8 = 44",1],["47 + 30 = 77",0],["59 + 4 = 63",1],["15 + 60 = 75",0],["68 + 5 = 73",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagadd100","caption":"Flagship lab: cut the ones to bridge a ten, add ten rods, find the jump to the next ten, sort sums by which digit changes, and write the jumps as an equation.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_cChv2j_-Da":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"A shop has {a} red pens and {b} blue pens. How many pens is that?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Split both numbers into tens and ones. Do the ones make another ten?","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b}. The ones {=a%10} + {=b%10} made more than 9, so one more ten joins the tens.","params":{"a":[33,66],"b":[18,29]},"where":"a%10+b%10>=10 && a+b<100"},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"Work out {a} + {=10*t-1}.","answer":"a+10*t-1","hint":"{=10*t-1} is 1 less than {=10*t}. Add {=10*t}, then take 1 away.","explain":"{a} + {=10*t} = {=a+10*t}, then take back the extra 1: {=a+10*t-1}.","params":{"a":[21,59],"t":[2,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Mo works out 68 + 29 as 68 + 30 + 1 = 99. What is the slip?","choices":["30 is 1 too many, so take 1 away: 97","Mo should have added 20","nothing, 99 is right"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is 30 more or less than 29? Which way should the fix go?","explain":"30 is 1 more than 29, so adding 30 adds 1 too many. Take it back: 98 − 1 = 97."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which is the quickest way to work out 73 − 68?","choices":["count up from 68 to 73","count back 68 ones from 73","split 68 into 60 and 8 and take each away"],"answer":0,"hint":"Are 73 and 68 close together?","explain":"68 to 70 is 2, 70 to 73 is 3, so the gap is 5. Counting back 68 ones would be very slow."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Ali says 54 − 27 = 33. What went wrong?","choices":["Ali did 7 − 4 in the ones instead of taking a ten","Ali added instead","nothing, 33 is right"],"answer":0,"hint":"Check it: does 33 + 27 make 54?","explain":"33 + 27 = 60, not 54. You cannot take 7 from 4, so take a ten: 14 − 7 = 7 and 4 tens − 2 tens = 2 tens. 54 − 27 = 27."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which addition checks that 91 − 46 = 45?","choices":["45 + 46","91 + 46","45 + 91"],"answer":0,"hint":"Add the answer to the number that was taken away.","explain":"45 + 46 = 91, which is where we started, so 91 − 46 = 45 is right."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"What is {a} − {b}?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Can you take {=b%10} from {=a%10}? If not, take a ten first. Check by adding back.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b}. Check: {=a-b} + {b} = {a}.","params":{"a":[52,94],"b":[15,38]},"where":"a%10<b%10"},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"46 + 37: why is the answer in the 80s, not the 70s?","choices":["6 + 7 = 13, which holds another ten","you always round up","37 is close to 40"],"answer":0,"hint":"Add the ones on their own. Is the total more than 9?","explain":"40 + 30 = 70, and 6 + 7 = 13. 13 is 1 ten and 3 ones, so 70 + 13 = 83."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What is the quickest way to work out 57 + 39?","choices":["add 40, then take 1 away","count on 39 ones from 57","add 30, then add 10 more"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is 39 close to a whole ten? How far from 40 is it?","explain":"39 is 1 less than 40. 57 + 40 = 97, then take back the extra 1: 96."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Work out {a} + {b}.","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the tens, add the ones, then join them. Watch for an extra ten in the ones.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b}. Tens: {=a-a%10} + {=b-b%10}. Ones: {=a%10} + {=b%10}. Then add the two totals.","params":{"a":[24,58],"b":[16,39]},"where":"a%10+b%10>=10 && a+b<100"},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Work out {a} − {=10*t-1} by taking away {=10*t} and then adjusting.","answer":"a-10*t+1","hint":"Take away {=10*t}. You took 1 too many, so give 1 back.","explain":"{a} − {=10*t} = {=a-10*t}, then add 1 back: {=a-10*t+1}.","params":{"a":[61,95],"t":[2,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"What is {=b+d} − {b}? Count up from {b}.","answer":"d","hint":"How far from {b} up to the next ten? Then how far from there to {=b+d}?","explain":"From {b} up to {=b+d} is {d}, so {=b+d} − {b} = {d}.","params":{"b":[56,88],"d":[2,6]},"where":"b%10>=5"},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Sam says 83 − 25 = 62. Which check shows the slip?","choices":["62 + 25 = 87, not 83","83 + 25 = 108","62 − 25 = 37"],"answer":0,"hint":"Adding undoes taking away. Add the answer to the number taken away.","explain":"62 + 25 = 87, which is not 83, so 62 is wrong. Sam did 5 − 3. The right answer is 58, because 58 + 25 = 83."}],"explain":{"kid":["Look at the numbers first, then pick a quick way. One way is to split into tens and ones. For 46 + 37, add the tens: 40 + 30 = 70. Add the ones: 6 + 7 = 13. Then 70 + 13 = 83. The 13 has one more ten in it.","Round and adjust. 29 is one less than 30. So 68 + 29 is 68 + 30, take 1 away. That is 97. For 83 − 19, take away 20, then give 1 back. That is 64.","Count up when the numbers are close. For 91 − 87, go from 87 up to 90. That is 3. Then 1 more to 91. So the gap is 4.","Check by adding back. If 83 − 25 = 58, then 58 + 25 must make 83. It does, so the answer is right."],"adult":["Ages 7 to 8. This is CCSS 2.NBT.5 (fluently add and subtract within 100 using strategies based on place value, properties of operations, and the relationship between addition and subtraction) and the NC Year 2 and Year 3 mental calculation statements. Key words: partition, tens and ones, compensation (round and adjust), difference, counting up (complementary addition), inverse.","Fluency here is flexibility plus accuracy, not speed alone. Partitioning works for any pair and leans on place value. Compensation uses a near multiple of ten and then corrects the over- or under-shoot. Counting up treats subtraction as finding the difference, which is fastest when the two numbers are close. The inverse check turns every take-away into an addition the child already trusts.","Common mistakes: losing the extra ten when the ones total more than 9 (46 + 37 = 73); writing the parts side by side (713); adjusting the wrong way after rounding (68 + 29 = 99, or 83 − 19 = 62); taking the smaller ones digit from the larger regardless of order (83 − 25 = 62, since 5 − 3 = 2); and using one slow method, such as counting back in ones, for every question.","In the lab your child drags 40, 6, 30 and 7 into tens and ones boxes, taps a jump of 30 or 20 on a number line and then fixes it by 1, drags a dot to count up from 87 to 91, sorts six sums by the quickest strategy, and checks Sam's 83 − 25 = 62 by adding 62 + 25 = 87, then drags the answer to 58."]},"familyTip":"When you see two prices, ask 'What's the quickest way?' before anyone works it out. For £68 + £29, say 'add 30 and take 1 back'. For 'how much more is 91p than 87p?', count up together: 3 to 90, 1 more to 91. Then ask your child to check a take-away by adding back.","keyIdea":"Being fluent within 100 means choosing a quick, reliable way for the numbers in front of you. Split both numbers into tens and ones when no number is near a ten (46 + 37 = 70 + 13 = 83). Round and adjust when one number is 1 or 2 from a ten (68 + 29 = 68 + 30 − 1 = 97; 83 − 19 = 83 − 20 + 1 = 64). Count up when two numbers are close (91 − 87: 87 → 90 → 91 is 4). Then check with the inverse: 83 − 25 = 58 because 58 + 25 = 83.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Check each answer by adding back. Drag it, or tap it and then a box.","opts":{"bins":["adds back to the start: right","does not add back: a slip"],"items":[["83 − 25 = 58",0],["64 − 28 = 44",1],["70 − 36 = 34",0],["52 − 17 = 45",1],["91 − 46 = 45",0],["75 − 38 = 43",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagflu100","caption":"Flagship lab: split into tens and ones, round and adjust, count up a small gap, pick the quickest strategy, check with the inverse.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_yJmvUCCym7":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"{a} frogs sit on a log. {b} jump into the water. How many frogs are still on the log?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Some frogs go away. Take {b} away and count the frogs that stay.","explain":"{a} take away {b} is {=a-b}. Fewer frogs are left on the log.","params":{"a":[6,9],"b":[2,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"{a} red cars and {b} blue cars. How many cars in all?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Put the two groups together. Count every car once.","explain":"{a} and {b} make {=a+b} cars in all.","params":{"a":[3,6],"b":[2,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"5 kids play. 2 more kids come. How many kids now?","choices":["7","3","2"],"answer":0,"hint":"More kids come. Will there be more or fewer than 5?","explain":"More came, so add: 5 and 2 more make 7 kids."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which story is a take-away story?","choices":["4 balloons. 1 pops.","4 balloons. 1 more is blown up.","4 red and 1 blue balloon"],"answer":0,"hint":"In a take-away story, some go away.","explain":"A balloon that pops goes away, so there are fewer. 4 take away 1 is 3."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"7 birds. 3 fly away. Sam says the answer is 3. What did Sam count?","choices":["the birds that flew away","the birds that are left","all the birds"],"answer":0,"hint":"The question asks how many are left on the fence.","explain":"3 is how many flew away. The question asks how many are left: 4."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"3 cats. 4 more cats come. Which number sentence fits?","choices":["3 + 4 = 7","4 − 3 = 1","3 − 4"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do cats come or go? Start with 3.","explain":"More cats come, so add. Start with 3: 3 + 4 = 7."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"9 sweets. 1 is eaten. Is 10 a sensible answer?","choices":["No, there must be fewer than 9","Yes","No, there must be more than 10"],"answer":0,"hint":"A sweet was eaten. More or fewer than 9?","explain":"Eating takes a sweet away, so there are fewer than 9. 9 take away 1 is 8."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"How can you work out a word problem if you are stuck?","choices":["act it out with toys or draw it","add the two numbers every time","guess a big number"],"answer":0,"hint":"What did we do with the birds and ducks?","explain":"Acting it out or drawing it shows if things come or go. Then you can count the answer."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"7 birds sit on a fence. 3 fly away. How many birds are left?","choices":["4","3","10"],"answer":0,"hint":"Some birds go away. Will there be more or fewer? Count the ones that stay.","explain":"3 fly away, so there are fewer. 7 take away 3 is 4 birds left."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"{a} ducks swim in a pond. {b} more ducks come. How many ducks now?","answer":"a+b","hint":"More ducks come, so there are more. Start at {a} and count on {b}.","explain":"{a} add {b} makes {=a+b}. More came, so the answer is more than {a}.","params":{"a":[2,7],"b":[1,3]},"where":"a+b<=10"},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"There are {a} cakes. {b} are eaten. How many cakes are left?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Eaten means they go away. Take {b} away from {a}.","explain":"{a} take away {b} leaves {=a-b} cakes. Fewer are left than at the start.","params":{"a":[5,10],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"6 apples. 2 are eaten. Which number sentence tells the story?","choices":["6 − 2 = 4","6 + 2 = 8","2 − 6"],"answer":0,"hint":"Start with how many there were. Do apples come or go?","explain":"Start with 6. 2 go away, so take away: 6 − 2 = 4."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"8 birds. 2 fly away. Pip says there are 10 birds now. Is Pip right?","choices":["No, there should be fewer than 8","Yes, 10 is right","No, there should be more than 10"],"answer":0,"hint":"Birds flew away. Should there be more or fewer than 8?","explain":"Birds went away, so there must be fewer than 8. Pip added. 8 take away 2 is 6."}],"explain":{"kid":["A word problem is a story with numbers. You can act it out with toys or draw it.","7 birds sit on a fence. 3 fly away. Now there are fewer birds. Count the birds that stay: 4. So 7 take away 3 is 4.","5 ducks swim. 3 more come. Now there are more ducks. Count them all: 8. So 5 add 3 is 8. 4 red apples and 2 green apples make 6 apples in all.","Ask: do more come, or do some go? Then check your answer. If some go away, the answer must be less than the start."],"adult":["Ages 4 to 6. This is CCSS K.OA.2 (solve addition and subtraction word problems, and add and subtract within 10, by using objects or drawings to represent the problem) and NC Year 1 (solve one-step problems that involve addition and subtraction, using concrete objects and pictorial representations). Key words: add, take away, altogether, how many left, more, fewer, number sentence. The problem types are add to (result unknown), take from (result unknown) and put together (total unknown).","Young children solve these by modelling the action: they make the starting set, then join or remove objects, then count. That is why the lab has them move the birds and ducks themselves before naming the operation. The question 'do more come, or do some go?' links the story structure to + or −, and writing the number sentence in story order (start, change, result) builds the bridge to symbols.","Common mistakes: answering with the number that changed (3 flew away, so '3'); adding every pair of numbers whatever the story says (7 birds, 3 fly away, '10'); counting only one part of a put-together set; writing the numbers in the wrong order (2 − 6); and recounting an object twice. A quick 'more or fewer at the end?' check catches most of these.","In the lab your child taps birds to make 3 fly away and counts the 4 that stay, drags 3 ducks into a pond of 5, taps 6 apples to count them once each, sorts six stories into 'more' or 'fewer', builds 6 − 2 = 4 from cards, and checks whether Pip's answers make sense on a number track."]},"familyTip":"Turn snack time into number stories. 'You have 6 grapes. You eat 2. How many are left?' Let your child move the real grapes, then say the answer. Swap roles: your child tells you a story and you solve it. Sometimes give a silly answer and let them catch it: 'I ate 2, so now there are 8!'","keyIdea":"A word problem is a little story with numbers. Act it out with objects or a quick drawing, then ask: do more come, or do some go away? More coming means add (5 ducks, 3 more come: 5 + 3 = 8). Some going away means take away (7 birds, 3 fly away: 7 − 3 = 4). Putting two groups together also means add (4 red and 2 green apples: 6 in all). Check the answer makes sense: if some went away, there must be fewer than at the start.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Do more come, or do some go? Drag each story, or tap it then a box.","opts":{"bins":["more come: add","some go: take away"],"items":[["3 more dogs run in",0],["2 cookies are eaten",1],["Dad brings 4 more cups",0],["5 leaves blow away",1],["1 more child sits down",0],["3 bubbles pop",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagstory","caption":"Flagship lab: make birds fly away, bring ducks in, count apples, sort stories, build 6 − 2 = 4, and check Pip's answers.","opts":{"step":0}}}});
